Below are the changes for the current release.
See the CHANGES file for changes in older releases.
See the RELEASENOTES file for a summary of changes in each release.
Issue # numbers mentioned below can be found on Github. For more details, add
the issue number to the end of the URL: https://github.com/swig/swig/issues/

Version 4.6.0 (in progress)
===========================

2026-11-11: wsfulton
            [Python] #3542 Add typecheck typemaps for std::filesystem::path. Overloaded
            functions taking a path only accepted a wrapped std::filesystem::path and now
            accept a str or pathlib.Path too. A str is passed to an overload taking a string
            ahead of one taking a path.

2026-11-11: wsfulton
            [Python] #3541 Passing None for a std::filesystem::path parameter crashed the
            interpreter. TypeError is now raised.

2026-11-11: wsfulton
            [Python] #3541 An exception raised when creating the pathlib.Path for a returned
            std::filesystem::path was replaced by a SystemError when the function also has an
            output parameter, such as int *OUTPUT. The exception is now propagated.

2026-11-11: alonfaraj
            [Python] #3541 Fix crashes in the std::filesystem::path typemaps. Passing a str
            that cannot be encoded as UTF-8, such as a filename that is not valid UTF-8, now
            raises UnicodeEncodeError, as does a pathlib.Path holding one except on Windows.
            Returning a path that is not valid UTF-8 now raises UnicodeDecodeError and an
            exception raised by the __str__ of a pathlib.Path subclass is now propagated.
            A reference leak when a pathlib.Path fails to convert on Windows is also fixed.

2026-11-09: vincentcouvert
            [Scilab] #3150 Fix std::string being an incomplete type when std_basic_string.i is
            included before std_string.i, which gave warning 402 for classes derived from
            std::string.

2026-11-08: wsfulton
            The typemap search for a type with both const and volatile qualifiers now strips
            volatile first, so a typemap for the const type is used before one for the type
            without qualifiers. Previously both were stripped in one step. For example, a
            parameter of type:

              const volatile int &iii

            used the 'int &' typemaps, which wrap it as a pointer in most target languages,
            rather than the 'const int &' typemaps, which pass it by value. The search now
            looks for 'int const &iii' and 'int const &' before 'int &iii' and 'int &'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-08: wsfulton
            [C] A pointer to a const volatile builtin type, such as 'const volatile int *', is
            now declared as 'const int *' rather than 'int *' in the C and C++ wrappers, so it
            keeps its const.

2026-11-07: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers in the .h file not compiling for a parameter that is a volatile
            class passed by value, or a pointer or a reference to one, such as those of:

              void f(volatile Foo v, volatile Foo *p, const volatile Foo &r);

            The C++ wrappers now take and return them without volatile, as 'Foo const &', 'Foo *'
            and 'Foo const &', as the other target languages do.

2026-11-07: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers in the .h file not compiling for a global variable, struct
            member or parameter that is an array of pointers to an enum or an array of arrays of
            enums, such as:

              enum Finger { One, Two };
              Finger *finger_ptrs[3];
              Finger finger_grid[2][3];

            The C++ wrappers now leave them out with warning 761, as for such arrays of a class.

2026-11-07: wsfulton
            [C] When wrapping C++, fix the wrappers not compiling for an array of arrays other than
            a two dimensional array of a builtin type, for a global variable or struct member that
            is an array of pointers to a class and for a struct member that is an array of classes,
            such as:

              int cube[2][3][4];
              Foo foos[2][4];
              Foo *ptrs[3];
              struct Holder { Foo items[2]; };

            It is now passed and returned as a pointer to its first element, as other arrays are.

2026-11-07: wsfulton
            [C] When wrapping C++, fix the wrapper of a function with a parameter that is an array
            of pointers, such as:

              int count(char *strs[3]);

            The wrapper freed the array passed to it, so passing an array that C code declared
            crashed or corrupted the heap. The array is no longer freed.

2026-11-07: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] #142
            Fix the wrapper not compiling for a global variable or struct member that is an array of
            three or more dimensions, such as:

              double x[2][3][4];

            Setting the variable now copies all of its elements.

2026-11-06: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling as C before C23 for a bool used only in a global
            variable or struct member declared in the .h file when wrapping C code, such as:

              extern bool flag;
              struct Options {
                bool visible;
              };

            The same went wrong for a global function pointer when wrapping C or C++ code:

              extern int (*callback)(bool);

            The .h file now includes stdbool.h for all of these, as it does for a bool parameter.

2026-11-04: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a two dimensional array of a builtin type passed to a wrapper function or to the
            setter of a global or member variable when wrapping C++ code, such as the parameter of:

              int sum_grid(const int grid[2][3]);

            The wrapper freed the array passed to it, so passing an array that C code declared
            crashed or corrupted the heap. The array is no longer freed.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [D] Fix a const member function declared through a function typedef, such as m in:

              typedef int G(double) const;
              struct S {
                G m;
              };

            It was wrapped as a D method that is not const, unlike a const member function declared
            directly. It is now a D const method.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            Fix the $decl and $fulldecl special variables for a function declared through a function
            typedef, such as f in:

              typedef int F(int);
              F f;

            $decl gave just the name and $fulldecl gave the declaration of a variable, 'int f'. They
            now give 'f(int)' and:

              int f(int)

            A member function declared through a function typedef also gets the cv-qualifier and
            ref-qualifier of the typedef, as in 'S::m(double) const'.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            The $decl and $fulldecl special variables and the declarations in warning messages left
            out the exception specification of a function, constructor or destructor, such as the
            'noexcept' of fp in:

              struct S {
                static int (*fp(int) noexcept)(double);
              };

            $fulldecl gave 'int (*S::fp(int))(double)' and now gives:

              int (*S::fp(int) noexcept)(double)

            A 'noexcept(expr)' or 'throw' exception specification is given too. The list of C/C++
            prototypes in the error for wrong arguments to an overloaded function in target
            languages such as Python has the exception specifications as well.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] Fix a director method returning a reference to a member pointer, or a
            non-const reference to a function pointer, such as getMemberPtrCref() in:

              struct Funcs {
                int increment(int x);
              };
              struct MemberPtrDirector {
                virtual int (Funcs::*const &getMemberPtrCref())(int);
              };

            The C++ code of the director class did not compile, as the variables for the value
            returned were declared with the name after the type, as in
            'static int (Funcs::*)(int) result_default ;'.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [Python] Fix the $pytypename special variable in a 'pytyping' typemap for a pointer to
            a member function with a cv-qualifier or a ref-qualifier, such as the parameter f in:

              %typemap(pytyping) SWIGTYPE (CLASS::*) "$pytypename";
              struct Funcs {
                short CC(bool) const;
              };
              int take_c(short (Funcs::*f)(bool) const);

            It gave SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__f_bool__short, the name for a pointer to a non-const member
            function, so the annotation of f named the wrong class. It now gives
            SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__q_const__f_bool__short, the runtime type of f. The same goes for
            $&pytypename and $*pytypename.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] Fix the wrappers
            of a static constexpr pointer to a const data member or to a member function with a
            cv-qualifier, a ref-qualifier or a const return type, such as cdp, cpp and csp in:

              struct Funcs {
                short CC(bool) const;
                const char *SS(bool) const;
                int data;
              };
              struct Holder {
                static constexpr const int Funcs::*cdp = &Funcs::data;
                static constexpr short (Funcs::*cpp)(bool) const = &Funcs::CC;
                static constexpr const char *(Funcs::*csp)(bool) const = &Funcs::SS;
              };

            The wrappers did not compile, as the constants were given their types without these
            qualifiers, such as 'short (Funcs::*)(bool)' for cpp. The constants now keep their
            types.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [C, C#, Guile, Java] Fix the type of a static constexpr pointer to a member function
            with a cv-qualifier or a ref-qualifier, such as cpp in:

              struct Funcs {
                short FF(bool);
                short CC(bool) const;
              };
              struct Holder {
                static constexpr short (Funcs::*cpp)(bool) const = &Funcs::CC;
              };
              int take_c(short (Funcs::*f)(bool) const);
              int take_f(short (Funcs::*f)(bool));

            The constant had the type of a pointer to a non-const member function,
            SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__f_bool__short, so it could not be passed to take_c and could be
            passed to take_f. It now has the type SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__q_const__f_bool__short of the
            parameter of take_c.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] Fix a const reference to a member pointer, such as m in:

              struct Shape {
                virtual double area();
              };
              double do_op_cref(Shape *s, double (Shape::*const &m)());

            The parameter took the type wrapper class of a pointer to the member pointer,
            SWIGTYPE_p_m_Shape__f_void__double, so a member pointer, which has the class
            SWIGTYPE_m_Shape__f_void__double, could not be passed for m. Passing null for m threw an
            exception. A function or variable of such a type gave a pointer to the member pointer,
            which could not be passed for a member pointer. A const reference to a member pointer is
            now passed and returned as the member pointer with the class of the member pointer, as
            a const reference to a pointer is passed and returned as the pointer. null is passed as
            a NULL member pointer and a NULL member pointer is returned as null. A director method
            gets and returns such a reference as the member pointer too.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [PHP] Add the names that PHP 8.0 to PHP 8.5 added to the keywords, reserved class names,
            built-in constants and built-in functions that SWIG warns about and renames, such as the
            class Override and the function array_first(). A wrapped class with the name of a PHP
            class replaced the PHP class and a wrapped function with the name of a PHP function
            stopped the module from loading. Such a class or function is now renamed with a c_
            prefix and warning 314 is given, as for the names that were already listed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [PHP] Fix the type declarations of a member pointer parameter or return value, such as
            for m and areapt in:

              double do_op(Shape *s, double (Shape::*m)(void));
              double (Shape::*areapt())(void);

            They named the PHP class SWIG\_m_Shape__f_void__double, but a member pointer is passed
            and returned as an object of class SWIG\_p_m_Shape__f_void__double, so reflection showed
            a type that the member pointer objects are not instances of. They now name the class of
            the member pointer objects.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [R] Fix a crash passing an object for a member pointer, such as an S object passed for
            the parameter of:

              struct S {
                int f(int);
              };
              int call(int (S::*m)(int));

            The object was read as the member pointer and R crashed. A member pointer read back
            with readRDS() crashed too. A SWIG type error is now given for these as for any other
            value that is not a member pointer, such as a number or NULL.

2026-11-03: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix a const reference to a member pointer, such as m and the return type in:

              struct Shape {
                virtual double area();
              };
              typedef double (Shape::*AreaFunc)();
              double do_op_cref(Shape *s, double (Shape::*const &m)());
              const AreaFunc &area_cref();

            Its Go type was a pointer to the Go type of a member pointer. Passing the address of a
            member pointer for m panicked with "argument of cgo function has Go pointer to unpinned
            Go pointer" and what area_cref() returned could not be used as a member pointer. A const
            reference to a member pointer is now passed and returned as the member pointer, with the
            same Go type, SwigMemberPtr, as a const reference to a pointer is passed and returned as
            the pointer. A director method gets and returns such a type as the member pointer too.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Guile, Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix overload dispatch on a parameter that is a const reference to a void pointer, as in:

              char *fvoidcref(int);
              char *fvoidcref(void *const &);

            Such a parameter takes any pointer, as a 'void *' parameter does, but calling fvoidcref
            with a pointer of a type other than 'void *' failed with an error saying that no
            overload matches. The overload taking the 'void *const &' is now called.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Lua] Fix a parameter that is a const reference to a void pointer, such as in:

              char *fvoidcref(void *const &);

            Only a 'void *' was accepted for it. Any pointer is now accepted, as for a 'void *'
            parameter, including in overload dispatch.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Perl, Ruby, Tcl] Fix a crash when passing the wrong kind of value for a
            member pointer, such as for m in:

              double do_op(Shape *s, double (Shape::*m)());

            Passing a pointer to an object in Tcl, a pointer to a type with no proxy class in Perl,
            a string that starts with an underscore but is not a member pointer in Ruby or null in
            Javascript with the node or napi engine crashed. So did calling an overloaded function
            with one of these when an overload takes a member pointer. A type error is now raised,
            or the overload that takes the value is called.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Guile, Javascript, Lua, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix a const reference to a member pointer, such as m in:

              struct Shape {
                virtual double area();
              };
              double do_op_cref(Shape *s, double (Shape::*const &m)());

            Passing a member pointer for m failed with a type error, as a pointer to the member
            pointer was expected. A function or variable of such a type gave a pointer to the
            member pointer, which could not be passed for a member pointer. A const reference to a
            member pointer is now passed and returned as the member pointer, with its type checked,
            as a const reference to a pointer is passed and returned as the pointer. A director
            method gets and returns such a type as the member pointer too.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix %rename being ignored by the C++ wrappers of member functions, static member
            functions and member variables, such as in:

              %rename(NewMethod) Foo::OldMethod;
              struct Foo {
                int OldMethod();
              };

            The C++ wrapper class declared the member with its C++ name, OldMethod, while the C
            function and the C++ wrappers of global functions and variables have the name given by
            %rename. The C++ wrappers of these members now have the name given by %rename too:

              int NewMethod();

            An operator keeps its C++ name. A static member function added with %extend also gets
            its own name instead of the name of the function that SWIG generates to implement it.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the generated code not compiling as C++ for a declaration of C++ code that is
            given a name that is a C++ keyword, such as by:

              %rename(new) create;

            The C++ wrapper used the name as it is, as in 'inline int new()'. So did the C
            declaration of a constant, as in '#define new 12', or, without -namespace, of a class,
            enum or enumerator. Such a declaration is now renamed by adding a 'c_' prefix to its
            C++ name, with a warning, as the other target languages rename their own keywords:

              Warning 314: 'new' is a C++ keyword, renaming to 'c_create'

            The C function is then named after c_create, as is the C++ wrapper. C code is not
            renamed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            Fix a g++ -Wextra warning, "type qualifiers ignored on cast result type", in the
            generated code for a const pointer to member, such as the parameter of:

              struct A {
                int f(int);
              };
              int call(int (A::*const mp)(int));

            whose wrapper did:

              result = (int)call((int (A::*const)(int))arg1);

            The top level const is now left out of the cast, as it already was for other types.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Go, Java, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby]
            Fix the director method of a virtual method declared noexcept(false), such as:

              struct Base {
                virtual int ping() noexcept(false);
              };

            It was declared noexcept in the director class, so an exception thrown by the director
            method, such as the one for an exception raised by the override in the target language,
            called std::terminate() instead of reaching the caller. It is now declared
            noexcept(false), like the method.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers crashing when NULL is passed for a pointer to a class, such as
            for the parameter of:

              int count(Foo *f);

            or to the setter of a member variable that is a pointer to a class. The C++ wrapper
            called a member function through the pointer to get the pointer to pass to the C
            function. NULL is now passed on to the C function, as NULL returned by the C function is
            returned by the C++ wrapper.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers not compiling for a parameter or variable that is an array of a
            class, such as the parameter of f() in:

              struct Foo {
                int x;
              };
              void f(const Foo a[3]);

            The C++ wrapper was declared with the array type before the name, as 'Foo const [3] a'.
            It now takes a pointer to the C++ wrapper class, as the C function takes a pointer to
            the first element of the array:

              void f(Foo const * a);

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file of a module importing another one not compiling as C99. It
            repeated typedefs of the .h file of the other module, which it includes, while C99
            does not allow a typedef to be repeated, unlike C11. With -c++, these were:

              typedef struct SwigObj SwigObj;

            and the typedef of a class that neither module wraps but both use, such as Opaque in:

              class Opaque;
              void use_opaque(Opaque *o);

            which is:

              typedef struct SWIGTYPE_p_Opaque SWIGTYPE_p_Opaque;

            Each of these typedefs is now declared once.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [PHP] A member pointer constant was not wrapped, so ADD_BY_VALUE was not defined for:

              struct Funktions {
                int addByValue(const int &a, int b);
              };
              %constant int (Funktions::*ADD_BY_VALUE)(const int &, int) = &Funktions::addByValue;

            Member pointer constants are now wrapped in the same way as a member pointer returned by
            a function.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [PHP] The type of a member pointer passed by value was not checked. A member pointer of
            another type or any other object was accepted and its bytes were copied as the member
            pointer. Passing null crashed. These now raise a TypeError.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix passing nil for a member pointer, such as for m in:

              struct Shape {
                double area();
              };
              double do_op(Shape *s, double (Shape::*m)());

            This crashed and now passes a NULL member pointer. A NULL member pointer returned from
            C++, such as by a function, a variable or a %constant, was not nil and is now nil. So is a
            NULL member pointer passed to a director method.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Add support for pointers to members, which could not be used, as in:

              struct Shape {
                double area() const;
              };
              double call(const Shape *s, double (Shape::*m)() const);
              double (Shape::*area_pointer())() const;

            The .h file declared them with a C++ type, as in 'double (Shape::*carg2)()', which is
            not valid C and loses the const, while the wrapper function took a pointer, which
            cannot hold a pointer to member function. The C++ wrappers left them out. Two overloads
            differing only in the type of a pointer to member function had the same name. A
            pointer to member, or a const reference to one, is now passed and returned by value as
            an opaque struct holding its bytes, which the C++ wrappers use too:

              typedef struct SWIGTYPE_m_Shape__q_const__f___double {
                void *swig_data[4];
              } SWIGTYPE_m_Shape__q_const__f___double;

              double example_call(Shape* carg1,SWIGTYPE_m_Shape__q_const__f___double carg2);
              SWIGTYPE_m_Shape__q_const__f___double example_area_pointer();

            A variable that is a pointer to member is wrapped by accessor functions and a %constant
            by a getter function. Any other pointer or reference to a pointer to member is an
            opaque pointer. The name of an overload now has the parameter and return types of a
            pointer to member function parameter instead of just 'f'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers not compiling for a conversion operator, such as in:

              %rename(toFoo) Bar::operator Foo();
              %rename(toFooRef) Bar::operator Foo&();
              struct Bar {
                operator Foo();
                operator Foo&();
              };

            The C++ wrapper class declared them with a return type, as in 'Foo operator Foo&();'.
            A conversion operator is now wrapped by a conversion operator to the type that its C++
            wrapper returns, keeping 'explicit', while its C function has the name given by
            %rename, such as Bar_toFoo. If another one already converts to this type, as both
            return a Foo object here, or if the type is a function pointer, it is wrapped by a
            member function with the name given by %rename instead. So the C++ wrapper class of Bar
            declares:

              operator Foo();
              Foo toFooRef();

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers not compiling for an instance of a member function template,
            such as:

              struct Calculator {
                template<typename T> T cube(T x) const { return x * x * x; }
              };
              %template(cube_int) Calculator::cube<int>;

            The C++ wrapper class declared it with its C++ name, as 'cube< int >', which is not
            valid in a declaration. It is now declared with the name given by %template, as the C
            function is:

              int cube_int(int x) const;

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix a director method taking or returning a member pointer, such as:

              %feature("director") MemberPtrDirector;
              class MemberPtrDirector {
              public:
                virtual ~MemberPtrDirector();
                virtual int useMemberPtr(int (Funcs::*m)(int), int x);
                virtual int (Funcs::*getMemberPtr())(int);
              };

            Go could not override these methods, as the Go type of a member pointer, _swig_memberptr,
            was not exported. A C++ call of getMemberPtr panicked with "result of Go function ...
            called from cgo is unpinned Go pointer", even when Go did not override it, as the member
            pointer was returned in Go memory. The Go type of a member pointer is now SwigMemberPtr. A
            member pointer returned by a Go director method is now copied to C++ memory.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix the wrapper for a parameter with an 'in' typemap of your own that takes no
            input, such as:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) int &level (int temp) "temp = 1; $1 = &temp;"
              void set_level(int &level);

            It did not compile, as the argout typemap for 'int &' was also used. It writes the value
            back through the input using a local variable of the 'in' typemap for 'int &'. The
            argout typemaps for 'int &', 'int &&' and 'std::string *' and the goargout typemap for
            'std::string *' are now only used with the 'in' typemap for the same type and not when
            it takes no input. If an 'in' typemap of your own relied on one of them to write the
            value back, add an argout typemap for it.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a const reference to a pointer, such as in:

              void set(Struct *const &s);
              Struct *const &get();

            It took and returned a pointer to the pointer, so passing a Struct object to set() gave
            a Failure exception, as did using the Struct object returned by get(). It is now passed
            and returned as the pointer, as in the other target languages, including in a director
            method.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a variable of an unnamed enum type, such as x here, which did not compile:

              enum { A, B } x;

            As there is no OCaml enum type for an unnamed enum, the variable is now an int, as a
            member variable of an unnamed enum type is.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a director method returning a reference, such as Ref() here:

              %feature("director") Base;
              struct Base {
                virtual DoubleHolder &Ref(DoubleHolder &x);
                virtual ~Base();
              };

            The reference returned to C++ was a NULL reference whatever the method returned in
            OCaml, so C++ crashed using it, even when the method was not overridden in OCaml. The
            reference returned in OCaml is now passed to C++. C_void returned for a reference gives
            an Invalid_argument exception.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a pointer or reference parameter accepting any C_string without a type check,
            such as a member pointer value for f or m in:

              struct Funcs {
                short FF(bool);
              };
              typedef short (Funcs::*FuncsPtr)(bool);
              short call(Funcs *f, FuncsPtr &m);

            The text of the string was used as the object pointed to, so using it could crash. A
            C_string is now accepted only for a string or a void * parameter, so a member pointer
            value passed for any other pointer or reference gives a Failure exception. A non-const
            reference to a member pointer takes a pointer to a member pointer, as in the other
            target languages.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Lua] Fix calling an overloaded function with nil when one of the overloads takes a
            member pointer, such as memberpt_or_pointer in:

              struct Funktions {
                int addByValue(const int &a, int b);
              };
              int memberpt_or_pointer(int (Funktions::*d)(const int &, int));
              int memberpt_or_pointer(Funktions *f);

            The overload taking the member pointer was chosen for nil, which it does not accept,
            so the call failed with an error such as "expected 'int (Funktions::*)(int const &,int)'
            got 'nil'". The overload taking the pointer is now called.

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Guile] Fix a pointer passed or returned by const reference, such as:

              Foo *const &get();
              void set(Foo *const &foo);

            It was wrapped as a pointer to the pointer, so the Foo returned by get could not be
            used and set could not be passed a Foo. It is now wrapped as the pointer, as in the
            other target languages. The same goes for a global or static member variable of such a
            type, except that a const reference to a char pointer is wrapped as a string, as is the
            result of a function returning one.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix a global or static member variable that is a const reference to a pointer, such as:

              Foo *const &foo_ref = foo_ptr;
              char *const &str_ref = str_ptr;

            It was wrapped as a pointer to the pointer, so the Foo it points to could not be used
            through it, nor could it be passed to a function taking a 'Foo *'. It is now wrapped as
            the pointer it refers to, or as a string for a char pointer such as str_ref, the same
            as the result of a function returning that type.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-02: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix a function returning a const reference to a function pointer and a global or static
            member variable of that type, such as get_fn_ref and fn_ref in:

              typedef int (*FnPtr)(int, int);
              const FnPtr &fn_ref = fn_ptr;
              const FnPtr &get_fn_ref();

            The wrapper of get_fn_ref did not compile. The wrapper of fn_ref did not compile either
            or wrapped it as a pointer to the function pointer. Both are now wrapped as the function
            pointer they refer to.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            Fix the declarations that the -addextern option adds for a function with an exception
            specification, such as:

              extern int (*f() noexcept)(int);
              extern int (*g() throw(int))(int);
              extern int h() throw(Foo *);

            The exception specification was left out or put after the parameter list of the
            function pointed to. The type in a 'throw' was not a C++ type, as in 'throw(p.Foo)'.
            The declarations gave compiler warnings or errors as a result. Now the exception
            specification is kept and goes after the parameter list of the declared function.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            Fix a template parameter qualified by const or volatile in a template instantiated
            with an array type, as in the parameter type of take in:

              template <typename T> struct Holder {
                int take(const T &);
              };
              %template(HolderInt3) Holder<int [3]>;
              %template(HolderConstInt3) Holder<const int [3]>;

            The wrapper of take for Holder<const int [3]> did not compile. The parameter of take
            for Holder<int [3]> was wrongly wrapped as a const reference to a pointer, using the
            typemaps for 'SWIGTYPE *const &'. The const now qualifies the elements of the array, as
            for a typedef of an array, so both parameters use any typemaps for 'const int (&)[ANY]'
            or else the default typemaps for a reference to an array.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            Fix the local variables of a typemap for a function pointer, named or not, such as:

              %typemap(in) int (*)(int) (int temp) { ... }
              %typemap(in) int (*f)(int) (int temp) { ... }

            The locals were parsed as part of the pattern, so the typemaps were registered for the
            types 'int (*)(int)(int)' and 'int (*f)(int)(int)' with no locals and were silently
            never used. The same went for a reference to a function, a pointer to a member function
            and an array of function pointers.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            Fix the qualifiers after a declarator that is not the first in a declaration, such as
            those of b, PB and g in:

              int (*a)(int) noexcept, (*b)(int) noexcept;
              typedef int (C::*PA)(int) const, (C::*PB)(int) const;
              struct S {
                int f() &, g() &&;
              };

            They were not handled as for the first declarator. b was a pointer to a function that
            is not noexcept, so the wrapper for setting a member variable like b did not compile
            from C++17. PB was a const pointer to a member function that is not const. g lost its
            rvalue ref-qualifier, so it was wrapped rather than ignored and the wrapper did not
            compile. Each declarator now gets the type and qualifiers it gets as the first one.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling as C99 when a type that SWIG does not wrap is used
            more than once, as in:

              struct Opaque;
              Opaque *opaque_pointer();
              int opaque_value(Opaque *o);

            The opaque struct of the type was declared each time it was used, so the .h file
            repeated this typedef, which C99 does not allow, unlike C11:

              typedef struct SWIGTYPE_p_Opaque SWIGTYPE_p_Opaque;

            It is now declared once.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the builtin types other than void, bool, char, short, int, long, float and
            double, such as unsigned int, signed char and long long, not being handled as the
            builtin types they are when wrapping C++ code. For example, given:

              unsigned int count;
              struct Arrays {
                unsigned int uints[2];
              };
              unsigned int value(unsigned int ***p);

            The member array was wrapped as an array of pointers. Its setter took an
            'unsigned int **' and wrote pointers into the array, beyond its end on a 64 bit
            platform. It also freed the pointer passed to it. It now takes an 'unsigned int *' and
            copies the values, as for an int array. The parameter of value was declared with
            'SWIGTYPE_p_p_p_unsigned_int *' and is now declared as 'unsigned int ***'. Without
            -namespace, the global variable is now declared in the .h file, as an int variable is,
            instead of being wrapped with accessor functions.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C declarations in the .h file when wrapping C++ code for a const reference
            to a pointer to a builtin type and for a pointer to a pointer to an enum, such as in:

              enum Colour { RED, GREEN };
              int int_value(int *const &p);
              int colour_value(Colour **c);
              int colour_reference_value(Colour *&c);

            The wrapper of int_value took a pointer to the pointer, but it was declared as taking a
            'const int **', which C code passing an 'int **' did not compile with. A const reference
            to a pointer to a builtin type, such as 'int *const &' or 'const int *const &', is now
            passed and returned as the pointer, as in the other target languages and as any other
            pointer passed by const reference already is. This changes the declarations of int_value
            and of the functions of a container of such pointers, such as:

              %template(IntConstPtrVector) std::vector<const int *>;

            in a module named example, from:

              int example_int_value(const int **carg1);
              void example_IntConstPtrVector_push_back(example_IntConstPtrVector *carg1, const int **carg2);
              const int **example_IntConstPtrVector_get(example_IntConstPtrVector *carg1, int carg2);

            to:

              int example_int_value(int *carg1);
              void example_IntConstPtrVector_push_back(example_IntConstPtrVector *carg1, const int *carg2);
              const int *example_IntConstPtrVector_get(example_IntConstPtrVector *carg1, int carg2);

            so C code passes the pointer, p, instead of its address, &p. The C++ wrappers take and
            return the pointer too.

            The other two functions were declared as taking an 'enum Colour *', losing a pointer, as
            did the C++ wrappers. They are now declared as taking an 'enum Colour **' and the C++
            wrappers take 'Colour **' and 'Colour *&'. They also take an array of enums as a pointer
            to the enum instead of as the enum. A typedef of a pointer or a reference to an enum is
            now taken and returned as the type it stands for, as in the C declarations.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the wrappers of std::map not compiling for a mapped type without a default
            constructor, such as NoDefault in:

              struct NoDefault {
                NoDefault(int v);
              };
              %template(NoDefaultMap) std::map<int, NoDefault>;

            The set function used operator[], which needs a default constructor. It now uses
            insert_or_assign when the C++17 standard library provides it, as the other target
            languages do.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C#, Java] Fix a memory leak each time a member pointer was passed between C++ and the
            target language, such as to a function, to set a variable or to a director method. The
            copy of the string holding the member pointer was never freed.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] Fix passing null for a member pointer, which crashed in D and threw a
            NullReferenceException in C# or a NullPointerException in Java, as did a director
            method overridden to return null for a member pointer. A NULL member pointer is now
            passed, as a NULL pointer is for a pointer. A NULL member pointer returned from C++,
            or passed to a director method, was an object and is now null.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] Fix pointers to member functions that differ only in their cv-qualifier
            getting the same proxy class. Given:

              struct Funcs {
                short FF(bool);
                short CC(bool) const;
              };
              int aaa1(short (Funcs::*)(bool) const);
              int bbb1(short (Funcs::*)(bool));

            the parameters of aaa1 and bbb1 were both of the class SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__f_bool__short,
            so a pointer to Funcs::FF could be passed to aaa1 and a pointer to Funcs::CC to bbb1.
            The parameter of aaa1 is now of the class SWIGTYPE_m_Funcs__q_const__f_bool__short and
            likewise for a pointer or reference to such a member pointer, which was of the class
            SWIGTYPE_p_m_Funcs__f_bool__short and is now SWIGTYPE_p_m_Funcs__q_const__f_bool__short.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            #2087 Fix the syntax error for a noexcept function type in an abstract declarator, such
            as that of an unnamed parameter, an alias declaration, a template argument or a
            trailing return type:

              void f(int (*)(int) noexcept);
              using FP = int (*)(int) noexcept;
              %template(HolderFP) Holder<int (*)(int) noexcept>;
              auto g() -> int (*)(int) noexcept;

            The noexcept is now part of the type, as for a named parameter. It is now also part of
            the type of a function typedef and of an alias declaration with a trailing return type,
            which dropped it, so the wrappers for a parameter of type MFP here did not compile:

              typedef int F(int) noexcept;
              using MFP = auto (C::*)(int) const noexcept -> int;

            A named parameter of a function pointer type with a noexcept or cv-qualifier after its
            parameter list can now have a default argument, which was a syntax error:

              void h(int (*p)(int) noexcept = nullptr);

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            Fix the qualifiers of the types in a template instantiated with a volatile type, such
            as the parameter types in:

              template <typename T> struct Holder {
                int take(const T &);
                int take_volatile(volatile T &);
              };
              %template(HolderVolatileInt) Holder<volatile int>;
              %template(HolderConstVolatileInt) Holder<const volatile int>;

            The volatile of Holder<const volatile int> was lost from the parameter of take, which
            was wrapped as 'const int &'. For Holder<volatile int> it did not match a typemap for
            'const volatile int &'. The wrappers of take_volatile cast to 'int volatile volatile &'
            and 'int const volatile volatile &', which do not compile. Now the qualifiers are
            merged. The parameter of take is 'const volatile int &' in both and uses the typemaps
            for it, which by default are not those of 'const int &'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [Guile, Javascript, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl]
            Fix a pointer to a member function being accepted for a pointer to a member function
            that differs only in its cv-qualifier. Given:

              struct Funcs {
                short FF(bool);
                short CC(bool) const;
              };
              int aaa1(short (Funcs::*)(bool) const);

            a pointer to Funcs::FF was accepted for the parameter of aaa1 and a pointer to Funcs::CC
            for a parameter of type 'short (Funcs::*)(bool)'. Both are now rejected with a type
            error, as is any pointer to a member function for one with a different cv-qualifier.

2026-11-01: wsfulton
            [R] Fix passing an R integer, such as 1L, for a double parameter. R failed with the
            error "REAL() can only be applied to a 'numeric', not a 'integer'". Likewise an R
            function passed for a function pointer failed when it returned a value of another R
            type than the C return type, such as a number, which R stores as a double, for an int.
            Such a value is now converted to the C type.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [R] Fix the check that an R function passed for a function pointer can take its
            arguments accepting any R function when the function pointer type had been used
            before, such as in a typedef. The R function then failed when it was called with
            "error in calling R function as a function pointer". Now it is rejected before the
            wrapped function is called.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [R] Fix an R function passed for a function pointer returning a value that cannot be
            converted to the return type, such as two integers for an int. In C++ R was terminated
            by an uncaught C++ exception. In C the C code was given an unconverted value and R
            warned of a stack imbalance. Now the R function gives an R error, such as
            "SWIG:TypeError in method '_p_f_int_int__int', argument 0 of type 'int'".

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [R] Fix calling an R function passed for a function pointer freeing all the memory
            given by R_alloc() to the code that called R. The R function optim() from the stats
            package, for example, keeps its work vectors in such memory while it calls the R
            function that it minimizes. Given a wrapped function taking a function pointer:

              int do_op(int a, int b, int (*op)(int, int));

            R could crash when the function minimized by optim() called it, as in:

              optim(c(1, 2), function(p) { do_op(1, 2, function(x, y) x + y); sum(p^2) })

            The memory is now kept until the code that allocated it releases it.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [R] Fix R functions passed for function pointer parameters, which could be called
            only once. A C function calling its function pointer twice failed with "Supposedly
            impossible error occurred in the SWIG callback mechanism". For a function with two
            function pointer parameters, the first called the R function passed for the second
            and the second called the one passed for the first. An R function that the C code did
            not call could be called later in place of another. An R function can now be called
            any number of times until the wrapped function returns. Calling it after that, such
            as through a function pointer that the C code kept, now gives an R error instead of
            calling some other R function or one that may have been freed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

