2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Remove a stray space from a const pointer to a function or array with no name in
            warnings and runtime error messages, which was shown as 'int (*const )(int,int)' and
            is now 'int (*const)(int,int)'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix a const reference to a typedef of a const pointer, such as 'const CFP &' given:

              typedef Foo *const CFP;

            The wrappers did not compile, as it was not wrapped as 'Foo *const &' by the
            'SWIGTYPE *const&' typemaps but as a const reference to 'Foo *const const'. It is
            now wrapped as 'Foo *const &' is. Likewise a const reference to a typedef of a
            volatile pointer is now wrapped as 'Foo *const volatile &' is. A template
            instantiated with 'const CFP' now uses an explicit or partial specialization for
            'Foo *const' and is the same instantiation as one with 'Foo *const', so
            instantiating both gives warning 404.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix the wrappers not compiling for a pointer to a member of a class in a namespace
            when the class is written without the namespace, as in the return type of memfuncptr in:

              namespace N {
                struct ABC {
                  int (ABC::*memfuncptr())(int) const;
                };
              }

            The wrappers used the type 'int (ABC::*)(int) const' and $fulldecl was
            'int (ABC::*N::ABC::memfuncptr())(int) const'. The class is now qualified, as in
            'int (N::ABC::*)(int) const'. This also changes the mangled name of such a type, so the
            type wrapper class of a member pointer to a class in a namespace is renamed, such as
            SWIGTYPE_m_N__ABC__int instead of SWIGTYPE_m_ABC__int for 'int N::ABC::*'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix '$fulldecl' for an unnamed enum. It gave the name that SWIG makes up for the enum in
            the declaration of a variable of the enum and two spaces after 'enum' in the
            declaration of an enumerator. For example, for:

              enum { EA, EB } unnamedenumvar;

            it gave 'enum $unnamed1$ unnamedenumvar' and 'enum  EA' and now gives
            'enum unnamedenumvar' and 'enum EA'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix the wrapper of a member function with both a cv-qualifier and a ref-qualifier, which
            called it on a non-const object, so it called an overload without the cv-qualifier
            instead, if there was one. For example, with:

              %rename(cl_const) X::cl() const &;
              struct X {
                int cl() &;
                int cl() const &;
              };

            cl_const called 'int cl() &' and now calls 'int cl() const &'. As SWIG does not evaluate
            constraints, an instantiation of such a C++20 member function template that does not
            satisfy its constraints now has to be ignored, as its wrapper no longer compiles, see
            the C++20 chapter.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

