2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a pointer to a class passed or returned by const reference using the wrong
            pointer, which could crash, given a class Foo, in:

              void set(Foo *const &p);
              Foo *const &get();

            The pointer itself is now passed and returned, as the .h file declares. Any other
            pointer passed or returned by const reference is now also passed and returned as the
            pointer, as in the other target languages, and the .h file declares it as it declares
            the pointer. For example, 'void *const &' was declared as 'void **' and is now declared
            as 'void *', and a function pointer passed by const reference, such as
            'int (*const &)(int)', is declared as 'int (*)(int)'. A pointer to a class that is not
            wrapped, such as X given only:

              struct X;

            passed as 'X *const &', was declared as 'SWIGTYPE_p_p_X *' and is now declared as
            'SWIGTYPE_p_X *'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix an rvalue reference to a function used as a parameter or return type, such as:

              typedef int Fn(int);
              int call(int (&&fn)(int), int i);
              int call_typedef(Fn &&fn, int i);
              int (&&get())(int);

            The wrapper code did not compile in most target languages, as it took ownership of the
            function from the target language and deleted it after the call. It is now wrapped in the
            same way as a reference to a function, so a function pointer constant or the result of
            get() can be passed to call().

