            [OCaml] Fix a compile error in a director class template with more than one template
            parameter and an ignored virtual method, such as ignored() here:

              %feature("director") Base;
              %ignore Base::ignored;
              template <typename X, typename Y> class Base {
              public:
                virtual int ignored() { return 1; }
                virtual ~Base() {}
              };
              %template(BaseII) Base<int, int>;

            The call to the base class method was passed to the CAMLreturnT macro, which took the
            comma in 'Base< int,int >' as a separator between its arguments. The call is now in
            parentheses.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a parameter that is a const reference to a member pointer, such as m in:

              struct Shape {
                virtual double area();
              };
              double do_op(Shape *s, double (Shape::*const &m)());

            Any C_string was accepted for it with no type check and the text was read as a member
            pointer, so passing a member pointer value could crash. It is now passed as a member
            pointer value with its type checked, as a const reference to a simple type is passed as
            a value. A const reference to a member pointer returned from a function or variable is
            now a member pointer value instead of a C_ptr to the member pointer. A director method
            gets and returns such a type as a member pointer value too.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] When wrapping C code, the generated header now declares a struct or union that SWIG
            has not seen defined before a wrapper function taking a pointer to it, such as Tree,
            which is only declared in a %{ %} block, in:

              %{
              struct Tree;
              %}
              void clear_tree_flags(struct Tree *tp, int i);

            The struct was first named in the parameter list of the wrapper function, giving a
            compiler warning that it is not visible outside it. A pointer to the struct declared
            by the caller then had an incompatible pointer type for the wrapper function.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the declarations of C structs and unions in the generated header when wrapping
            C code, which did not match the wrapper functions, for example for:

              typedef struct Size { int width; int height; } Size;
              typedef union Value { int i; double d; } Value;
              struct Point { int x; int y; };
              void draw(struct Point *p, Size *s, Value *v);

            The header declared 'typedef struct {...} Size;' without the tag, but the wrapper
            function with a 'struct Size *' parameter, so C code calling it got an incompatible
            pointer type error. A union was declared as a struct, so it did not have the layout of
            the union. With the -namespace option, 'struct Point' was declared as
            'struct ns_Point'. The header now declares them as in the original code.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C declarations in the .h file when wrapping C++ code for a pointer to a
            pointer of a builtin type, which had a pointer too many, and for a variable that is an
            array of arrays of unknown size, which was a syntax error, such as:

              int tri(int ***p);
              const int arr[][3] = { {1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6} };

            The parameter of tri was declared with 'int ****' and is now declared with 'int ***'.
            The getter of arr was declared as returning 'int [][3]*' and now returns 'int *'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C declarations in the .h file when wrapping C++ code losing a pointer of a
            pointer to a pointer to a class or of a reference to a pointer to a class, such as:

              struct Struct { int value; };
              Struct **make_ptr_ptr();
              int pointer_reference_value(Struct *&s);

            These were declared with 'mod_Struct*' with -namespace mod, but the wrappers return
            and take a pointer to the pointer. They are now declared with 'mod_Struct **'. The same
            is fixed for a const volatile reference to a pointer, as in 'Struct *const volatile &'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file declaration of a global variable or struct member of a function
            pointer type using an enum that SWIG does not know, as it is only declared in a %{ %}
            block, such as:

              %{
              enum E { EA };
              enum EE { EEA };
              %}
              enum E (*fp)(enum EE);

            The declaration was the invalid 'SWIGIMPORT int (*fp)(intE);'. An unknown enum is now
            declared as int in the parameters of a function pointer too, giving:

              SWIGIMPORT int (*fp)(int);

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix two dimensional arrays in the .h file when wrapping C code, for example for:

              int grid[2][3];
              int sum_grid(int a[2][3]);

            The global variable was declared as 'SWIGIMPORT int grid[];'. The parameter used
            'int **' instead of the pointer to an array that the wrapper uses, as did the accessor
            functions of the variable with -namespace, so C code using them did not compile. They
            are now declared as:

              SWIGIMPORT int grid[][3];
              SWIGIMPORT int grid_sum_grid(int (*carg1)[3]);

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the members of a struct declared in the .h file when wrapping C code losing
            their pointer, array or function pointer declarators, for example for:

              struct Members {
                int *ip;
                int (*fp)(int);
                int arr[4];
              };

            The .h file declared 'int ip; int fp; int arr;', so the struct had a different layout
            from the one the wrappers were compiled with. A function pointer returning void gave
            'void fptr;', which did not compile. The members are now declared as in the original
            struct.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file declaring a struct passed or returned by value as a pointer to it
            when wrapping C code, for example for:

              typedef struct { int myInt; short myShort; } test;
              test get_test();
              int get_my_int(test t);

            The .h file declared 'test *mod_get_test()' and 'int mod_get_my_int(test *carg1)' for
            a module named mod, but the wrappers pass and return the struct by value, as the C
            functions do. The .h file now declares them by value too.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file dropping the inner qualifiers of a pointer type when wrapping C code,
            for example for:

              int point_x(const struct Point *p);
              int colour_value(const enum Colour *const *c);

            The parameters were declared as 'struct Point *carg1' and 'enum Colour* carg1', so C
            code passing a pointer to a const struct got a discarded qualifier warning. A pointer
            to a pointer to an enum also lost a pointer. They are now declared as in the C
            function, as 'struct Point const *carg1' and 'enum Colour const *const *carg1'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the wrapper of a C function returning a pointer to a const pointer, such as:

              const char **names_get(void);
              const char *(*namer_get(void))(int);

            not compiling with gcc 14 and later when wrapping C code, with the error "returning
            'char **' from a function with incompatible return type 'const char **'". The result
            is now cast back to the return type.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling as C before C23 for a bool used only in a function
            pointer type, such as in:

              int call(bool (*fn)(bool), int i);

            The .h file now includes stdbool.h for it, as it does for a bool parameter.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a function pointer using a class that is not
            wrapped, as it is only declared or is ignored, such as:

              struct Opaque;
              void set_cb(void (*cb)(Opaque *));

            The parameter was declared as 'void (*carg1)(Opaque *)' and the C++ wrapper of set_cb
            did not compile. The class is now the opaque struct used for it elsewhere in the .h
            file, as in 'void (*carg1)(SWIGTYPE_p_Opaque *)', and the C++ wrapper is left out with
            warning 761.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a pointer or a reference to an array, such as in:

              int sum(int (*a)[4]);
              int sum_ptr_ref(int (*&a)[4]);
              int (&get_array())[4];

            These were declared with an 'int (*)[4]* carg1' parameter and as returning 'int [4]*',
            and are now declared with 'int (*carg1)[4]' and 'int (**carg1)[4]' parameters and as
            'int (*get_array())[4]'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a reference to a function, such as in:

              int call_ref(int (&f)(const int &, int), int a, int b);
              int (&get_ref())(const int &, int);

            These were declared with an 'int (int const &,int)* carg1' parameter and as returning
            'int (int const &,int)*', and are now declared with a pointer to the function, as in
            'int (*carg1)(int const *,int)' and 'int (*get_ref())(int const *,int)'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a reference in the parameters or return type of a
            function pointer, such as in:

              int call(int &(*op)(const int &, int), int a, int b);

            The parameter was declared as 'int &(*carg1)(int const &,int)'. As C has no references,
            each reference in a function pointer type is now declared as a pointer, as in
            'int *(*carg1)(int const *,int)', and the C++ wrapper casts the function pointer.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            #45 Fix a member function with its name in parentheses, as is done to stop a
            function-like macro of the same name expanding, such as:

              struct Foo {
                int (foo)(int i);
              };

            It was ignored with the warning below and is now wrapped in the same way as when its
            name is not in parentheses:

              Warning 504: Function Foo::int(foo) must have a return type. Ignored.

            The syntax error for 'const', 'noexcept' or '= 0' after the parameters of such a
            function is fixed, outside a class too. So are a function template and a function with
            a qualified return type, which were dropped without a warning, such as:

              std::string (bar)(int i);

            Outside a class, such a function now gets the overloaded wrappers for its default
            arguments.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for a reference to a function returning a pointer as a parameter or
            return type, for example:

              int callref(int *(&d)(int), int i);
              int *(&g())(int);

            These are now wrapped in the same way as when the function type is a typedef, as in
            'PIntFn &' given:

              typedef int *PIntFn(int);

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for a member function returning a pointer to a member function when
            the member function has a cv-qualifier or ref-qualifier, which is written inside the
            parentheses of the declarator, such as f here:

              struct C {};
              typedef int (C::*CMemFn)(int) const;
              struct S {
                int (C::*f() const)(int) const;
                CMemFn g() const;
              };

            f is now wrapped in the same way as g, which declares the same member function with a
            typedef for its return type.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for a member function returning a pointer or reference to a function
            when the member function has a 'noexcept' or 'throw' exception specification, which is
            written inside the parentheses of the declarator, such as f here:

              typedef int (*IntOp)(int);
              struct S {
                int (*f() const noexcept)(int);
                IntOp g() const noexcept;
              };

            f is now wrapped in the same way as g, which declares the same member function with a
            typedef for its return type.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] The C function names of some overloads change, as the mangled types of their
            parameters, which are appended to the names, no longer drop the closing parenthesis
            from an array size, such as for the parameter of:

              void f(char text[sizeof("abc")]);

            which was mangled as 'asizeof_Sp__S22_abc_S22_c' and is now
            'asizeof_Sp__S22_abc_S22__SP_c'. The mangled types of a reference to a function, a
            pointer to a pointer to a function and a pointer to a member of a class template
            instance change too, such as 'int (&)(int)', which was 'rf_Sp_inti' and is now
            'rf_ii'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the generated code not compiling for the overloads of a member function that
            differ only in their qualifiers, unless one is just the const overload of the other.
            For example:

              struct S {
                int g(int);
                int g(int) volatile;
                int h(int);
                int h(int) const volatile;
              };

            The overloads of each pair were given the same C function name. An overload now gets
            the '_const', '_volatile' and '_rvalue' suffixes in its C function name for each of
            its 'const', 'volatile' and '&&' qualifiers that another overload with the same
            parameters does not have. So the second overloads of g and h are now S_g_volatile_i
            and S_h_const_volatile_i.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrapper of a member function with both a cv-qualifier and a
            ref-qualifier, which was not a const method, so it could not be called on a const C++
            wrapper object. For example:

              struct Host {
                int cl() const &;
              };

            gave 'int cl();' in the C++ wrapper class Host and now gives:

              int cl() const;

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers of the accessor functions of a global variable without the
            -namespace option, which called themselves instead of the C accessor functions with the
            same names. For example:

              namespace space {
                int number = 1;
              }

            gave a C++ wrapper number_set() that crashed on infinite recursion. The C++ wrapper of
            the getter of a variable of class type did not compile. The C++ wrappers are now named
            after the variable, as they are with -namespace, so number(1) sets the variable and
            number() gets it.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix the $fulldecl special variable in the get and set wrappers of a variable used with
            %allowexception. A set wrapper gave the void return type and the variable name, such as
            'void ABC::funcptrvar' for:

              struct ABC {
                int (*funcptrvar)(int);
              };

            The get wrapper of a variable of class type gave a pointer or const reference type. Both
            wrappers now give the declaration of the variable, such as 'int (*ABC::funcptrvar)(int)'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix the $fulldecl special variable for a conversion operator, which gave a return type in
            front of the declaration, such as 'int ABC::operator int() const' for:

              struct ABC {
                operator int() const;
              };

            It now gives 'ABC::operator int() const'.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a pointer to a class with top level qualifiers, such as 'Foo *const' or
            'Foo *volatile', being wrapped as an object passed by value, as in:

              void constptr(Foo *const p);
              Foo *volatile vfoo;

            The C function for constptr read the 'Foo *' it was given as a pointer to a pointer and
            the getter of vfoo returned a pointer to a new copy of the pointer. When wrapping C, the
            header file declared the parameter of constptr as 'struct Foo **'. The C++ wrappers did
            not compile. These pointers are now wrapped as a 'Foo *', as the other target languages
            do for 'Foo *const'. A pointer to an enum with top level qualifiers, such as 'E *const',
            no longer makes SWIG fail an assertion when generating the C++ wrappers. A function
            pointer with top level qualifiers, such as 'int (*const f)(int)', is now declared in the
            header file as the function pointer. It was declared as a pointer to the function
            pointer when wrapping C and as 'int (**)(int)*', which did not compile, when wrapping
            C++.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix a g++ -Wextra warning, "type qualifiers ignored on cast result type", in the
            generated code for a variable or function parameter whose type has a top level
            volatile qualifier, such as for:

              volatile int vi;

            whose setter did:

              vi = (int volatile )arg1;

            The top level volatile is now left out of the cast, as a top level const already
            was.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [Java] Fix the generated code not compiling for a pointer to a class passed by const
