            It now takes an R function, as a function pointer written out in full does.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a const function pointer parameter or return type, such as in:

              int call_op(int (*const op)(int, int));
              int (*const get_op())(int, int);

            When wrapping C++, call_op took a pointer to the function pointer and had no C++
            wrapper, giving warning 761, and the wrapper of get_op, or of a member function
            returning a const function pointer, did not compile. When wrapping C, the .h file
            declared call_op and get_op as taking and returning a pointer to the function pointer,
            unlike the C functions. They now take and return the function pointer, as for a
            non-const function pointer, and have C++ wrappers.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a reference or a pointer to a function pointer,
            such as the parameters in:

              typedef int (*IntOp)(int, int);
              void set_op(IntOp &op);
              void set_op_ptr(IntOp *op);

            These were declared as 'int (*)(int,int)* carg1' or similar. As C has no references,
            they are now declared as a pointer to the function pointer, 'int (**carg1)(int,int)'.
            The same is fixed for an rvalue reference and for a function returning a reference or
            a pointer to a function pointer.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file not compiling for a function pointer that uses a class or an enum
            in its parameters or return type, such as:

              namespace ns {
                struct Foo {...};
                enum Color { RED, GREEN };
              }
              void set_cb(void (*cb)(ns::Foo *));
              void set_shader(ns::Color (*shader)(int));

            The C declarations used the C++ name of the class, as in 'void (*carg1)(ns::Foo *)',
            and declared set_shader as taking a pointer to the enum instead of the function
            pointer. They now use the C names of the class and the enum that the .h file uses
            elsewhere, as in 'void (*carg1)(mod_Foo *)' and 'enum mod_Color (*carg1)(int)' with
            -namespace mod. When wrapping C, a function pointer returning an enum was declared as
            a pointer to the enum too and is now declared as it is.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            Fix the typedefs in the parameters of a function pointer not being resolved when it
            returns a class or an enum in a namespace, such as after:

              namespace ns {
                enum Colour { Red, Green };
              }
              typedef int Index;
              ns::Colour (*get_colour_fn())(int);
              ns::Colour call_colour_fn(ns::Colour (*fn)(Index), Index i);

            The function pointer returned by get_colour_fn could not be passed to call_colour_fn,
            giving a TypeError in Python for example, as 'ns::Colour (*)(Index)' was not known to
            be the same type as 'ns::Colour (*)(int)', and a typemap for the resolved type did not
            match. The C#, D and Java classes for such function pointer types are now named after
            the resolved type, such as SWIGTYPE_p_f_int__ns__Colour instead of
            SWIGTYPE_p_f_Index__ns__Colour.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix member function pointers, which were cut short to the size of a pointer when
            passed between C++ and OCaml. A member function pointer is twice that size with GCC and
            Clang, so calling a member function through one could crash. A member pointer is now a
            C_string value holding the whole member pointer and its type name, instead of a C_ptr.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a compile error in a director method returning a class template type with
            more than one template argument, such as getPair() here:

              %feature("director") Base;
              template <typename X, typename Y> class Pair {};
              struct Base {
                virtual Pair<int, int> getPair();
              };

            The return type was passed to the CAMLreturnT macro, which took the comma in
            'Pair< int,int >' as a separator between its arguments. A local typedef for the return
            type is now used in the macro instead.

2026-10-31: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java] A director method returning a function pointer, such as getFuncPtr in:

              class FuncPtrClass {
              public:
                virtual int (*getFuncPtr(int which))(int, int);
              };

            gave Warning 473 "Returning a reference, pointer or pointer wrapper in a director method
            is not recommended." This warning is about the lifetime of an object returned by pointer
            or reference. A function pointer does not point to such an object, so the warning is no
            longer given, as in the other target languages.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for a reference to a function pointer as a parameter, return type or
            variable, for example:

              void ref_op(int (*&r)(int));
              int (*&getFuncPtrRef())(int, int);

            These are now wrapped in the same way as when the function pointer type is a typedef, as
            in 'IntOp &' given:

              typedef int (*IntOp)(int);

            An rvalue reference such as 'int (*&&r)(int)' is fixed too. So are a reference to a
            const pointer such as 'int (*const &r)(int)' and a reference to a pointer to an array
            such as 'int (*&a)[4]'.

            The exception is a director method returning a reference to a function pointer, which
            does not compile in some target languages unless the function pointer type is a typedef.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for a member function returning a pointer or reference to a function
            when the member function has a cv-qualifier or ref-qualifier, which is written inside
            the parentheses of the declarator, such as f here:

              typedef int (*IntOp)(int, int);
              struct S {
                int (*f() const)(int, int);
                IntOp g() const;
              };

            f is now wrapped in the same way as g, which declares the same member function with a
            typedef for its return type. So f is a const method. A member function with an '&&'
            ref-qualifier is ignored by default as usual.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            Fix the wrong operator->() being called for a const volatile method accessed through a
            smart pointer class that has both a const and a non-const operator->(), such as get()
            here:

              struct Foo {
                int get() const volatile;
              };
              struct FooPtr {
                Foo *operator->();
                const Foo *operator->() const;
              };

            The wrapper for get() through FooPtr called the non-const operator->(). It now calls
            the const operator->(), as it already did for a const method. The same goes for a const
            volatile method added to Foo with %extend.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            Fix the $fulldecl special variable for a function returning a function pointer, such as:

              struct ABC {
                static int (*funcptrmethod(int x))(int, int);
              };

            $fulldecl put the return type in front of the name, giving
            'int (*)(int,int) ABC::funcptrmethod(int)', and now gives the declaration as it is
            written in C++, 'int (*ABC::funcptrmethod(int))(int,int)'. The same is fixed for a
            variable with a function pointer or array type, such as 'int ABC::arr[10]' instead of
            'int [10] ABC::arr'. There is no longer a space between a pointer or reference return
            type and the name, so 'short * ABC::method(int)' is now 'short *ABC::method(int)'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [R] Fix the generated code not compiling for a function with a function pointer
            parameter whose return type is const, such as:

              const char *do_name(int i, const char *(*namer)(int));

            The C++ compiler gave an invalid conversion error, as the code that calls an R
            function passed as the callback returned 'const char *' while the parameter was held
            in a variable whose function pointer type returned 'char *'. The wrapper now compiles
            and an R function or a C function pointer can be passed for the parameter.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the generated code not compiling for a method with a volatile and a const
            volatile overload with the same parameters, such as:

              struct V {
                int f(int) volatile;
                int f(int) const volatile;
              };

            Both overloads were given the same C function name. The const volatile overload now
            gets the '_const' suffix in its C function name, as a const overload does.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers not compiling when the name of a struct or class is a part of
            'const' or 'volatile', such as 's' here:

              typedef struct s {...} S;
              int get_x(const S *p);

            The C++ wrapper class name S replaced every 's' in the parameter type, giving
            'S conSt *p'. Now only the type name is replaced, as in 'S const *p'.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] #3180 Fix the C++ wrappers leaving out a function, a method or a member variable with a
            function pointer parameter or return type, such as:

              class MyClass {
              public:
                void setCreateCallback(void *(*c_callback)(const char *json));
              };

            These gave warning 761 and were only available as C functions. The C++ wrappers now take
            or return the same function pointer as the C function, so a C or C++ function can be
            passed to setCreateCallback in the C++ MyClass class. A function pointer that uses a class
            or an enum in its parameters or return type still gives warning 761.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the .h file dropping the const in the return type of a function pointer, so
            passing a function returning 'const char *' to this function failed to compile:

              void set_namer(const char *(*namer)(int));

            The C function and its C++ wrapper took a 'char *(*)(int)' parameter and now take a
            'char const *(*)(int)' parameter. The same applies to a function returning such a
            function pointer and to a member variable of this type.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrappers in the .h file not using the names of the parameters, except
            the first one, such as 'y' here:

              int add(int x, int y);

            The C++ wrappers now use the parameter names of the wrapped function.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the C++ wrapper of a member function declared 'const volatile', which was not a
            const method, so it could not be called on a const C++ wrapper object. For example:

              struct S {
                int cv() const volatile;
              };

            gave 'int cv();' in the C++ wrapper class S and now gives:

              int cv() const;

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a director method returning a function pointer, a member function pointer or
            a pointer to an array failing to compile, as the type was passed to CAMLreturnT(), as in
            'CAMLreturnT(int (*)(int,int), ...)'. A typedef of such a type is now passed instead.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            [Perl, Python, Ruby] Fix the wrapper of a director method returning a function pointer
            failing to compile, as it released the ownership of the function pointer as if it were
            a pointer to an object.

2026-10-30: wsfulton
            Fix a director method returning a function pointer failing to compile, such as:

              %feature("director") FuncPtrClass;
              class FuncPtrClass {
              public:
                virtual int (*getFuncPtr(int which))(int, int);
              };

            The director class declared the method as 'int (*)(int,int)getFuncPtr(int which)' and
            now declares it as 'int (*getFuncPtr(int which))(int,int)'.

2026-10-28: wsfulton
            Fix the CMake build on macOS using the PCRE2 header from one install and the PCRE2
            library from another. With PCRE2 installed by Homebrew, the build compiled against the
            Homebrew header but linked against the older PCRE2 library that comes with macOS.
            'swig -pcreversion' then showed a different version to the one CMake reported. The
            library is now taken from the same install as the header.

2026-10-25: wsfulton
            Fix %apply giving no warning when it applies nothing. Warning 453 was not given if the
            last parameter of the pattern being applied had any typemaps, even ones that are not part
            of the pattern, such as the 'int' typemaps here:

              %apply (double *NOSUCH, int) { (double *arr, int len) };

            An existing %apply that never did anything now gives Warning 453:

              example.i:1: Warning 453: Can't apply (double *NOSUCH,int). No typemaps are defined.

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            Fix the 'warning' attribute of a typemap giving the same warning more than once for a
            parameter or function, such as 3 times in Python and up to 5 times in Go for 'x' here:

              %typemap(in, warning="901:in typemap used") int x "..."
              int f(int x);

            The warning is now given once for each parameter or function the typemap is used for.

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the declaration in the .h file of the wrapper of a C function with an 'in' typemap
            with numinputs=0, or a multi-argument 'in' or 'ctype' typemap, which left out parameters,
            such as 'ignored' here or the last parameter of memmove in cdata.i:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) int ignored "$1 = 10;"
              int ignore_first(int ignored, int b);

            The 'in' typemaps are not used when wrapping C, so the wrapper has all the parameters of
            the function, as its declaration now does too.

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            [Javascript] Fix the napi engine leaving out some check typemaps for a function with a
            multi-argument 'in' typemap, such as the check typemap for 'b' here:

              %typemap(in) (const char *s, int len) "..."
              %typemap(check) int len "..."
              %typemap(check) int b "..."
              void f(const char *s, int len, int b);

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            [R] Fix a crash for a parameter with an 'in' typemap of your own, such as this one, as the
            freearg typemap for its type freed memory that the 'in' typemap had not allocated:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) int *count (int temp) "$1 = &temp;"
              %typemap(argout) int *count "..."
              void get_count(int *count);

            For a string or number array parameter, such as 'char *names[3]' or 'int values[n]', the
            wrapper did not compile instead. The freearg typemaps for 'int *', 'double *' and the other
            number pointers, 'char *', 'char []' and these arrays are now only used with the 'in'
            typemap for the same type, as they free what it allocated. If an 'in' typemap of your own
            allocates memory that one of these typemaps freed, add a freearg typemap to free it.

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            The code of an 'in' typemap with numinputs=0 now comes in the wrapper in parameter order,
            after the code converting the earlier arguments. It came before any argument was
            converted, so it could not use their values, such as the runtime size of a variable
            length array parameter. An output array sized by an earlier parameter can now be
            allocated directly:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) double numbers[*] "$1 = (double *)calloc($1_dim0, sizeof(double));"
              %typemap(freearg) double numbers[*] "free($1);"
              void squares(int n, double numbers[n]);

2026-10-24: wsfulton
            [C, Lua, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, R, Ruby, Tcl] #3598 A 'freearg' typemap can now crash
            the wrapper if it assumes that the 'in' typemap with numinputs=0 for the same parameter has
            run. The 'freearg' typemaps also run when a call fails, such as for the wrong number of
            arguments or an earlier argument that cannot be converted. The 'in' typemap now comes after
            the code converting the earlier arguments, so it may not have run by then. A pointer
            parameter is then still NULL and most other types are not initialised. For example, with
            these typemaps, calling get_name with an invalid 'id' raised an error and now crashes:

              %typemap(in, numinputs=0) char **out (char *temp = 0) "$1 = &temp;"
              %typemap(freearg) char **out "free(*$1);"
              void get_name(int id, char **out);

            Such a typemap must check the pointer first, which also works with older versions of SWIG:

              %typemap(freearg) char **out "if ($1) free(*$1);"

            In Ruby, this only happens if an 'in' typemap of your own uses SWIG_fail.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

