            Fix '$1_dim10' to '$1_dim19' in typemaps, which were replaced as '$1_dim1' followed by a
            digit for an array with 11 or more dimensions.

2026-10-20: wsfulton
            Fix a 'throws' typemap that declares a local variable, which gave a wrapper that did not
            compile, for example:

              %typemap(throws) int (const char *message) {
                message = $1 == 1 ? "int 1 thrown" : "int thrown";
                SWIG_exception(SWIG_RuntimeError, message);
              }

            The local variable was not declared in the catch block that uses it. C#, D and Java also
            declared an unused local variable in the wrapper, such as 'message1', which is an error
            with -Werror. The local variable is now declared in the catch block.

2026-10-19: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the wrapper of a member inherited from a base class ignoring the %exception code and
            the exception specification of the base class member, for example:

              %exception Base::get {...}
              struct Base {
                int get();
              };
              struct Derived : Base {
              };

            An exception thrown by Base::get() was caught when calling Base_get() but not when calling
            Derived_get(), so it terminated the program. Derived_get() now uses the %exception code, the
            exception specification, %catches and the other features of the base class member, as
            Base_get() does.

2026-10-19: wsfulton
            [C] Fix the wrapper not compiling when wrapping C code for a function with an array
            parameter, such as:

              short sum_array(short x[5]);

            The wrapper declared the parameter as 'short [5] arg1' instead of 'short arg1[5]'.

2026-10-18: wsfulton
            [C] Fix %exception being ignored when wrapping C code, that is without the -c++ option,
            such as:

              %exception positive_result {
                $action
                if (result <= 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "positive result expected");
              }

            The wrapper called the wrapped function without the %exception code. %feature("action")
            and %contract code were ignored too. They are now used, as they are when wrapping C++ code.

2026-10-18: wsfulton
            [C] Fix wrappers that did not compile when wrapping C code, such as for:

              int counter;
              const int get_limit(void);

            The wrapper for a function returning a const type, such as get_limit(), assigned the result
            to a const variable. With the -namespace option, global variables such as counter are wrapped
            with accessor functions, which called the variable as if it were a function, as in
            counter(arg1). The wrappers now compile.

2026-10-18: wsfulton
            [C] The SWIG error codes, such as SWIG_ValueError, are now defined in the generated header, so
            C code can check the error code of a pending exception by name, for example:

              if (SWIG_CException_code_get(ex) == SWIG_ValueError) {...}

            Before, the number had to be used instead, such as -9.

2026-10-17: wsfulton
            [C] #3588 Fix a memory leak when an error is raised after an 'in' typemap has allocated memory.
            SWIG_exception left the wrapper without running the 'freearg' typemaps that release the memory.
            SWIG_exception now leaves the wrapper with the new SWIG_fail macro, which jumps to a fail label
            at the end of the wrapper. The code after the label runs the 'freearg' typemaps and then returns.
            SWIG_fail can also be used directly in a typemap to leave the wrapper after an error.

            A typemap using SWIG_exception or SWIG_fail must now have the canthrow attribute, which
            generates the fail label, for example:

              %typemap(check, canthrow=1) int positive {
                if ($1 <= 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "positive value expected");
              }

            Without it, the wrapper does not compile. Warning 762 is given if SWIG_exception or SWIG_fail is
            found in a wrapper without the canthrow attribute. The warning cannot be relied on though, as other
            ways of jumping to the fail label are not found, such as a macro that uses SWIG_fail.

            %exception and %exceptionvar have the canthrow attribute set, as do the library typemaps that
            raise an error.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-17: wsfulton
            [C#, D] Fix missing warning for SWIG_exception used without the canthrow attribute, such as
            in this typemap:

              %typemap(check) int number {
                if ($1 < 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "only positive numbers accepted");
              }

            The exception was not thrown, as the proxy method did not check for a pending exception,
            yet no warning was given. Now warning 845 (C#) or 711 (D) is given, as it already was for
            SWIG_CSharpSetPendingException and SWIG_DSetPendingException. Add canthrow=1 to fix it.

2026-10-17: wsfulton
            [C#, D] Fix %exceptionvar code that raises an exception when a variable is accessed, such as:

              %exceptionvar number {
                $action
                if (number < 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "number is negative");
              }

            Before, the variable's getter and setter did not check for the pending exception, so the
            exception was not thrown. Now it is thrown, as it is for %exception code.

