2026-10-16: wsfulton
            A typemap written with '*' as the size of an array, such as 'char [*]', is now used for a
            C99 variable length array parameter. Its '$1_dim0' is the size worked out at runtime from
            the earlier parameter:

              int char_length(int n, const char s[n]);

            The size is used even when the function is first declared without it, as C allows:

              int char_length(int, const char [*]);
              int char_length(int n, const char s[n]);

            In a multidimensional array, each dimension whose size is only known at runtime is written
            with '*', as in 'double [*][*]' for 'double m[r][c]'. A dimension with a constant size is
            written with its size or with ANY, as in 'double [*][ANY]' for 'double m[n][3]'. '$1_dim1'
            and so on are the sizes of the other dimensions and '$1_size' is all the sizes multiplied
            together.

            A typemap written with '*' is used before any typemap for an array of unknown size, even
            when the '*' typemap only matches once a qualifier is removed or a typedef is resolved. So
            'int [*]' is used rather than 'const int []' for 'const int a[n]'. The default typemaps are
            looked for in the same order, 'SWIGTYPE [*]' before 'SWIGTYPE []'.

            A typemap written with the size as declared, such as 'const char s[n]' or
            'const char [n]', is used before any typemap written with '*'. It is used in the same way,
            so its '$1_dim0' is the size worked out at runtime too. Its size has to be written as
            declared, so it does not match 'const char s[len]'.

            In a multi-argument typemap, an array written with '*', or with a size that refers to an
            earlier parameter of the typemap, only matches a variable length array parameter. Its
            '$2_dim0' and so on are the sizes of that array:

              %typemap(in) (int n, double values[n]) {...}
              double sum(int n, double values[n]);

            Such typemaps are provided for a char array in every target language except C, which passes
            the array on unchanged. In R, they are also provided for an array of numbers, enums or
            strings. They copy the string or vector into an array of that size, as for a fixed size
            array, instead of passing it without checking its length. A string or vector too long for
            the array is now an error.

            The typemaps for a fixed size array in arrays_java.i, arrays_javascript.i and carray.i are
            also used for a variable length array parameter, with the size it has at runtime, such as
            'n' for 'values' here:

              int sum(int n, const int values[n]);

            arrays_java.i throws an IndexOutOfBoundsException for an array that is not 'n' long.
            arrays_javascript.i reports an error for an array shorter than 'n', as carray.i does with
            Invalid_argument. For ARRAYSOFENUMS use:

              %apply ARRAYSOFENUMS[ANY] { name[ANY], name[*] };

            The size is only known once the arguments are converted. So it can only be used in the
            'in', 'check', 'argout' and 'freearg' typemaps. Using it anywhere else is an error, such
            as in a target language typemap or a typemap's local variables or attributes.

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Fix "metaclass conflict" TypeError when importing a module with a class using
            the nondynamic feature that derives from a nondynamic class from another module.

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Fix accessing the elements of a wrapped STL container of a class using the
            nondynamic feature, which raised AttributeError.

2026-10-16: vadz
            [Python] Setting a property of a class using the nondynamic feature no longer calls its
            getter, so it works even when the getter throws, e.g. because the value is not set yet.

2026-10-15: wsfulton
            A C99 variable length array parameter, which has a size that refers to an earlier
            parameter, is now wrapped as an array of unknown size, such as 'char []'. It was wrapped
            as a fixed size array, such as 'char [ANY]'. The wrappers did not compile for some types,
            such as these:

              int char_length(int n, const char s[n]);
              double trace(int n, double m[n][n]);

            Typemaps for a fixed size array are no longer used for such a parameter. A typemap
            written with the size as declared, such as 'const char s[n]', is still used.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-15: wsfulton
            Fix a template argument containing a '.', such as a floating point literal, a member
            access or a C++20 designated initializer, which was scrambled in the generated code so
            that it did not compile:

              struct Point { int x, y; };
              constexpr Point s{1, 2};
              template<double D> struct DoubleValue {...};
              template<int N> struct IntValue {...};
              template<Point P> struct PointValue {...};

              %template(D) DoubleValue<1.5>;            // was DoubleValue< 5 1. >
              %template(X) IntValue<s.x>;               // was IntValue< x s. >
              %template(P) PointValue<Point{.x = 1}>;   // was PointValue< x = 1} Point{. >

            Also fix a default argument value such as 's.y' becoming 's.int' when 'typedef int y;'
            is in scope and %feature("compactdefaultargs") is used.

2026-10-15: wsfulton
            Add support for C++11 default template arguments in function templates. %template can now
            leave out the template arguments that have defaults, as for a class template, such as:

              template<typename T, typename U = int> int value(T t) { return 1; }
              %template(value_int) value<int>;

            Previously %template gave the error "No matching function template" unless every template
            argument was given. For an overloaded function template, an overload needing a default was
            left out instead, with no error if another overload took the template arguments. Such an
            overload is now instantiated too, as C++ also considers it. Its wrapper does not compile if
            its body does not work for the template arguments, such as the first area in:

              template<typename T, typename U = void> int area(T *shape) { return shape->area(); }
              template<typename T> int area(T side) { return side * side; }
              %template(area) area<int>;

            which is now also instantiated as 'int area(int *shape)' using the default for U. Its body
            does not compile as int has no area() member. To wrap only the second area as before,
            ignore the first one:

              %ignore area<int>(int *);
              %template(area) area<int>;

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-15: wsfulton
            %template on an overloaded function template now instantiates only the overloads whose
            template parameters take the template arguments: a type for a type template parameter, a
            value for a non-type template parameter and a template for a template template parameter.
            Previously every overload with as many template parameters as template arguments was
            instantiated, whatever the kind of argument. The wrapper did not compile when the parameter
            or return types of such an overload used a template parameter given the wrong kind of
            argument, such as the value 10 for the type template parameter T in:

              template<typename T> T kindp(T t) { return t; }
              template<int N> int kindp(int i) { return N + i; }
              %template(kindp_10) kindp<10>;

            which also instantiated the first kindp as '10 kindp(10 t)'. A type for a non-type template
            parameter failed in the same way, such as int for N in an 'int (&a)[N]' parameter.

            A template parameter with a type not declared to SWIG, such as 'std::size_t N' without a
            typedef for size_t, takes any template argument, as the name could also be a concept
            constraining a type template parameter.

2026-10-15: wsfulton
            Function templates differing only in their template parameters are now overloads, as in C++.
            They were taken to be a redefinition, giving Warning 302 and ignoring the later function
            template, so %template instantiated the earlier one instead. For example:

              template<typename T> T make() { return T(); }
              template<int N> int make() { return N; }
              %template(make_5) make<5>;

            instantiated the first make as '5 make()', which did not compile.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            Add support for 'static', type qualifiers and '*' in the brackets of a function
            parameter's array, as allowed by C99, which were a syntax error.  They do not change
            the function's type, so they are ignored and the parameter is wrapped as if they were
            not there:

              double average(const double values[static 3]);
              void clear(int buffer[const 10]);
              int total(size_t, const int [*]);

            They are reported as an error for a global variable, structure member or typedef.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [R] Fix the wrapper for a function pointer type with an array parameter, which did
            not compile:

              typedef int (*summer)(const int a[3]);

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [C, C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, PHP] An exception caught by the catch all handler that
            %catches(...) adds is now raised as a SWIG_RuntimeError with the message "unknown exception",
            as in the other target languages. Before, it was rethrown with warning 468 unless the interface
            file included exception.i. To rethrow it as before, add this to the interface file:

              %typemap(throws) (...) "throw;"

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            %include <exception.i> is no longer needed and the file now does nothing. The 'throws' typemap
            for '...' used by %catches(...) is now always available. Every C++ wrapper now includes the
            <stdexcept> and <typeinfo> headers needed by SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT and SWIG_CATCH_UNKNOWN.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [Guile] A global variable that is an array of unknown size, such as 'extern int b[];' or
            'extern char name[];', now gives warning 462, as in the other target languages. Setting it
            still raises wrong-type-arg.

2026-10-14: wsfulton
            [Python] Fix a memory leak with -castmode of a std::pair made from a tuple whose first element
            is converted with a cast of higher rank than its second, such as a float for an int.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [R] NULL now selects an overloaded function taking a char array, such as 'char [4]' or
            'char []', as it does for any other array.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] A string now selects an overloaded function taking a
            reference to a char array, such as 'char (&)[4]' or 'char (&&)[4]', as it does for 'char [4]'.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Guile, Lua, PHP] A string now selects an overloaded function taking a char array, such as
            'char [4]', as it does for 'char *'. In Guile it now does for 'char []' too. In PHP, null now
            selects one too, as for any other array.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Setting a read only member or global variable now raises Failure "Unable to set 'x', as
            it is read only" instead of being silently ignored.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [R] Setting a read only member variable, or a name that is not a member variable, with $<- or
            [[<- now gives an error instead of a stack overflow ("C stack usage" or "node stack overflow").
            Getting a name that is not a member variable or method with $ now gives NULL, also instead of a
            stack overflow.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [PHP] Setting a read only member variable now throws an Error "Cannot modify readonly property
            Foo::$x", as for a PHP readonly property, instead of "Invalid callback , no array or string
            given".

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [C] Fix a derived class having a set function for an inherited read only member variable. The
            wrapper code did not compile for a const member variable and a member variable made read only
            with %immutable could be set.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Scilab] Remove the extra ';' after the gateway function in the generated code for Scilab 6 and
            later, which gives an "extra ';' outside of a function" warning with -Wextra-semi in C++.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Perl] Fix reading a global variable of pointer, array, reference or class type changing a
            pointer object assigned to it. After a failed set, such as setting an 'int *' variable to a
            'double *' pointer object, reading the variable changed that object to hold the variable's
            pointer and gave it as the variable's value. After a successful set, a later change to the
            variable in C changed the assigned pointer object too. The variable no longer holds a
            reference to the assigned pointer object, so, as in the other target languages, the object
            must be kept alive for as long as C uses the pointer.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Javascript] null for an array parameter using the arrays_javascript.i typemaps, such as
            'int []' or 'double [4]', is now passed on to the function as a NULL pointer, as for any other
            array parameter, instead of raising an error. Setting an array variable to null still raises
            an error. To raise an error for a null array parameter as before, add a check typemap for each
            array type, for example:

              %typemap(check) int[], int[ANY] %{
                if (!$1) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ERROR, "null array");
                }
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [Java] A null Java array for an array parameter using the arrays_java.i typemaps, such as
            'int []' or an array of classes from JAVA_ARRAYSOFCLASSES, is now passed on to the function as
            a NULL pointer, as for any other array parameter, instead of throwing a NullPointerException.
            Setting an array variable to null still throws a NullPointerException. To throw one for a null
            array parameter as before, add a check typemap for each array type, for example:

              %typemap(check) int[], int[ANY] %{
                if (!$1) {
                  SWIG_JavaThrowException(jenv, SWIG_JavaNullPointerException, "null array");
                  return $null;
                }
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            An rvalue reference to an array, such as 'int (&&)[4]', is now wrapped as a reference to an
            array, as a parameter or as a member or global variable. The wrapper code did not compile,
            except for an array of char in some target languages. As for a reference to an array, NULL
            gives a NULL reference error, an array of char is a string and no ownership is taken.

2026-10-13: wsfulton
            [C] NULL for a reference to a primitive type, bool or an enum, such as 'int &' or 'int *&', now
            gives a NULL reference error instead of passing a NULL reference to the wrapped function.

