2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, R, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT now catches
            std::bad_cast and raises SWIG_TypeError, as it already did in the other target languages,
            instead of SWIG_SystemError from catching std::exception. This is for better consistency of
            the error handling common to all target languages, which now has a single definition of
            SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT. To raise SWIG_SystemError as before, redefine SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT in the
            interface file:

              #undef SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT
              %define SWIG_CATCH_STDEXCEPT
                catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, e.what());
                } catch (std::domain_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, e.what());
                } catch (std::overflow_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_OverflowError, e.what());
                } catch (std::out_of_range& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_IndexError, e.what());
                } catch (std::length_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_IndexError, e.what());
                } catch (std::runtime_error& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_RuntimeError, e.what());
                } catch (std::exception& e) {
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_SystemError, e.what());
                }
              %enddef

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            SWIG_exception can now be used anywhere without %include <exception.i>, such as in a typemap or
            in %exception code, as its support code is now always included, for example:

              %typemap(check) int positive {
                if ($1 <= 0)
                  SWIG_exception(SWIG_ValueError, "Expected a positive value.");
              }

            SWIG_exception_fail is now available in every target language, as the same macro as
            SWIG_exception. This is part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the
            typemaps common to all target languages.

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [R] SWIG_exception now raises an R error for SWIG_IndexError, as for the other error codes,
            instead of returning NA. This is part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including
            in the typemaps common to all target languages. To return NA as before, redefine
            SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG
            library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { if ((code) == SWIG_IndexError) \
                return Rf_ScalarLogical(NA_LOGICAL); SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Ruby] SWIG_exception now jumps to the wrapper's fail label after raising the error, as
            SWIG_exception_fail does, so it can only be used in a wrapper function, as in the other target
            languages. As rb_raise does not return, it could be used elsewhere before, such as in the body
            of an %extend method or in a %{ %} function. This is part of a refactor for consistent error
            handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To use it elsewhere as
            before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which
            the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Javascript] With Node-API and NAPI_CPP_EXCEPTIONS, SWIG_exception now jumps to the wrapper's
            fail label after throwing the error, as SWIG_exception_fail does, so it can only be used in a
            wrapper function, as in the other target languages. As the error is a C++ exception, it could be
            used elsewhere before, such as in the body of an %extend method or in a %{ %} function. This is
            part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target
            languages. To use it elsewhere as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also
            redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_Error(code, msg)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Tcl] SWIG_exception now leaves the wrapper through its cleanup code, as SWIG_fail does, so any
            memory allocated for the arguments is freed. It returned TCL_ERROR directly before. This
            corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in
            the typemaps common to all target languages. To return directly as before, redefine
            SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library
            typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); return TCL_ERROR; } while (0)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Scilab] SWIG_exception now leaves the wrapper after setting the error. It carried on with the
            rest of the wrapper before. This corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for
            consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To carry
            on after the error as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file. Also redefine
            SWIG_exception_fail, which the SWIG library typemaps use, to keep its behaviour:

              %{
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_Scilab_Error(code, msg)
              #undef SWIG_exception_fail
              #define SWIG_exception_fail(code, msg) do { SWIG_Error(code, msg); SWIG_fail; } while (0)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [C] SWIG_exception now returns from the wrapper after raising the error. It carried on with the
            rest of the wrapper before, after raising the SWIG_CException when wrapping C++ code, or after
            calling any SWIG_exception defined in C by the user when wrapping C code or using -noexcept. This
            corrects the error handling as part of a refactor for consistent error handling, including in the
            typemaps common to all target languages. To carry on as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the
            interface file before using it:

              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_CException_Raise(code, msg)

            When wrapping C code or using -noexcept, only the #undef is needed.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [C#] SWIG_exception now throws an ArgumentNullException for SWIG_NullReferenceError, as the null
            checks in the C# typemaps do, instead of a NullReferenceException. This is part of a refactor for
            consistent error handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To throw a
            NullReferenceException as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file before using it:

              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) { if ((code) == SWIG_NullReferenceError) \
                SWIG_CSharpSetPendingException(SWIG_CSharpNullReferenceException, msg); \
                else SWIG_CSharpException(code, msg); return $null; }

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-11: wsfulton
            [Guile] SWIG_exception now raises an error with a symbol as its key, such as swig-type-error,
            so it can be caught by name. It used a string as the key before, which Guile does not accept,
            so a wrong-type-arg error was raised instead. SWIG_NullReferenceError now gives misc-error, as
            for the null checks in the Guile typemaps. This is part of a refactor for consistent error
            handling, including in the typemaps common to all target languages. To raise the same errors
            as before, redefine SWIG_exception in the interface file:

              %{
              SWIGINTERN void SWIG_exception_string_key(int code, const char *msg, const char *subr) {
                static const char *keys[] = { "swig-error", "swig-io-error", "swig-runtime-error",
                  "swig-index-error", "swig-type-error", "swig-division-by-zero", "swig-overflow-error",
                  "swig-syntax-error", "swig-value-error", "swig-system-error", "swig-error",
                  "swig-memory-error", "swig-null-reference-error" };
                const char *key = code <= -1 && code >= -13 ? keys[-code - 1] : "swig-error";
                scm_error(scm_from_locale_string(key), subr, msg, SCM_EOL, SCM_BOOL_F);
              }
              #undef SWIG_exception
              #define SWIG_exception(code, msg) SWIG_exception_string_key(code, msg, FUNC_NAME)
              %}

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-10: wsfulton
            [OCaml] An array, such as 'int [4]', 'Foo [2]' or 'int [2][3]', is now wrapped as a
            pointer to its first element by default, as in the other target languages. An array
            parameter, member variable or global variable failed with an error such as "Array
