            such as 'int values[4]'. Reading an integer or enum array failed with an R error such
            as '"_p_int" is not a defined class'. Reading a float or double array gave an R external
            pointer, which could not be used in R. The array is now read as an R vector of all its
            elements, of integers, numbers or enum names, which is also what is used to set it. For
            example, for a struct member 'int values[4]':

              s$values <- c(1L, 2L, 3L, 4L)
              s$values
              [1] 1 2 3 4

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] Fix a fixed size array parameter, member or global variable of a numeric type or an
            enum, such as 'int [4]', given a vector with fewer elements than the array, which read
            past the end of the vector. The remaining elements are now 0. A vector with more
            elements than the array now gives a SWIG OverflowError.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] Enum names, such as c("One", "Two"), can now be passed for an enum array parameter,
            such as 'enum Finger [2]', which gave the R internal error "INTEGER() can only be
            applied to a 'integer', not a 'character'". An integer vector is still accepted.

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] NULL for a pointer to or an array of a numeric type or an enum, such as 'int *',
            'double [3]' or 'int []', is now passed as a NULL pointer, as in the other target
            languages, rather than as a pointer to a zero length array, or for an array of enums,
            giving an R internal error.

2026-10-08: wsfulton
            [R] NULL can now be passed to an overloaded function or method taking a reference to
            a pointer, such as 'Foo *const &', which gave "cannot find overloaded function".

2026-10-07: olly
	    [Guile] #3194 The default "procdoc" format is now "plain" (which
	    works with Guile 1.4.1 and later), and the old "guile-1.4" format
	    is no longer supported.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [R] NULL for a primitive type, such as int, double or bool, passed by value or by
            const reference, or for a const reference to an enum, now gives a SWIG TypeError. It
            crashed for int, long and bool passed by value. It gave an R internal error, such as
            "REAL() can only be applied to a 'numeric', not a 'NULL'", for float and double passed
            by value and for a const reference to an enum. Otherwise it gave NA, or true for bool.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [R] NULL for a reference to a primitive or library type, such as 'int &',
            'std::string &' or 'std::vector<int> &', including the INPUT and INOUT typemaps for a
            reference, now gives SWIG:NullReferenceError, as for a reference to a class, instead
            of an R internal error, "R_ExternalPtrAddr: argument of type INTSXP is not an external
            pointer" in R 4.5. A number for the INPUT and INOUT typemaps for a reference, such as
            'int &INPUT', which gave the same R error, is now accepted.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Tcl] A negative number, such as -1, is no longer accepted for an unsigned long long
            parameter or variable, where it gave a very large number. An OverflowError is raised
            instead. Nor is a negative number with spaces before it, such as " -1", for an unsigned
            long. An unsigned long value too big for a long is now accepted in any form Tcl accepts
            for an integer, such as with spaces after it, as for a smaller value.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Perl] A negative number in a string, such as "-1", or a negative floating point number,
            such as -1.0, is no longer accepted for an unsigned long, unsigned long long or size_t
            parameter or variable, where it gave a very large number. An OverflowError is raised
            instead, as it already was for the integer -1.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Perl] An empty string or undef is no longer accepted as 0 for an integer or floating
            point parameter or variable, such as int or double. A TypeError is raised instead, as it
            already was for any other string that is not a number. This includes the value returned
            by a director method implemented in Perl, so a method that returns undef, such as with
            'return;', for an int now throws Swig::DirectorTypeMismatchException instead of giving 0.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [R] Fix the reverse order '(size_t LENGTH, const char *STRING)' typemaps. Also fix the
            '(const char *STRING, size_t LENGTH)' typemaps when applied to another char type, such
            as 'unsigned char *'. Both converted the string to an integer, so a string could not be
            passed.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby] Fix NULL, such as None in Python, passed for
            a string with a length from the '(const char *STRING, size_t LENGTH)' typemaps, the
            reverse order '(size_t LENGTH, const char *STRING)' typemaps or %cstring_input_binary.
            The NULL pointer was given a length of -1, converted to the length's type, so a size_t
            length was its largest value. The length is now 0.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [C#] Fix a constant from a wide string or wide character literal holding a character
            that is not ASCII, with %csconst(1), which was given a C# char for each byte of its
            UTF-8 encoding, so that a wide character constant did not compile.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            Fix the value of a wide string literal holding a character that is not ASCII, such as
            L"\x263A", L"\u00E9" or the character written as itself, used for a constant or
            default argument. A hexadecimal escape sequence above 0xFF was truncated to a single
            byte, including one beyond Unicode, such as L"\x110000", which a 32 bit wchar_t holds.
            An octal one above \377 gave an "octal escape sequence out of range" error. Any other
            such character became a wide character for each byte of its UTF-8 encoding. Also fix
            the array size deduced for an auto or decltype(auto) variable initialised by such a
            literal, such as 'auto &s = L"\u00E9";', which counted the UTF-8 bytes.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Guile] A char, signed char or unsigned char parameter or variable, including the
            elements of a list or vector from list-vector.i, now raises wrong-type-arg for
            anything other than a character, such as #nil, a number or a string, which was
            converted to an arbitrary char. It raises out-of-range for a character above 255, which
            was truncated.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [R] NULL is now accepted for a 'char *' parameter, member or global variable and gives
            a NULL pointer. NULL for a char parameter gives a SWIG TypeError. Both gave the R
            internal error "CHAR() can only be applied to a 'CHARSXP', not a 'NULL'" before. An
            overload taking 'char *' is now selected for NULL. Errors for NULL, such as an invalid
            null reference, are now reported as SWIG:NullReferenceError instead of
            SWIG:UnknownError.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [R] Fix a parameter that is a fixed size array of strings, such as 'char *strs[3]',
            which was given the bytes of the first string in place of the array of pointers,
            usually crashing. A character vector is now converted to the array, with any elements
            past the end of a shorter vector set to NULL. A longer vector is an error. NULL is
            passed as a NULL pointer. A member or global variable of this type, which was set the
            same way, now keeps copies of the strings, as for 'char *'.

2026-10-07: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby] Fix a crash when C++ calls a director method that has a
            char or wchar_t array parameter, such as 'char [4]', with NULL. The target language
            method is now passed what it is passed for a NULL 'char *'.

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Java, Javascript, Lua, OCaml, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby]
            Setting a char array member or global variable, such as 'char [8]', to NULL, such as
            None in Python, now raises an error, as there is nothing to copy. Previously the array
            was zero filled, or only its first char was cleared in some target languages.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby] NULL, such as None in Python, for a fixed
            size char array parameter, such as 'char [8]', is now passed on as a NULL pointer, as
            for any other array parameter, instead of as a zero filled array. In PHP NULL is also
            passed on for a 'char []' parameter, which was given an empty string.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [Javascript] Fix a crash with Node-API for a NULL 'char *' result, such as a function
            returning NULL. It now gives undefined, as with the other Javascript engines.

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby] NULL, such as None in Python, is no longer
            accepted for a char parameter, which was converted to '\0'. So with overloads for both
            char and 'const char *', NULL now calls the 'const char *' overload.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [Lua] Fix a crash when nil is passed for a char or 'const char &' parameter. An error
            is now raised instead.

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [R] NULL for a char array parameter, such as 'char [8]' or 'char []', is now passed on
            as a NULL pointer, as for any other array parameter, instead of giving an R error. NULL
            set on a char array member or global variable now gives a SWIG NullReferenceError
            instead of the R internal error "CHAR() can only be applied to a 'CHARSXP', not a
            'NULL'".

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            [C] When wrapping C++, setting a char array member or global variable, such as
            'char [8]', to NULL now calls SWIG_exception with SWIG_NullReferenceError, which raises
            a SWIG_CException, as there is nothing to copy. Previously only its first char was
            cleared.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-06: wsfulton
            Typemap code that is just "pass" is deprecated and now gives warning 128. Support
            for it will be removed in a future release.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [R] Fix reading a char array variable or member, such as 'char [4]' or 'char []', or
            a reference to a char array returned from a function, which failed with an error such
            as 'undefined class "_p_char"'. The string is now returned, as for 'char *'.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Perl] Setting a global variable to a value that cannot be converted to its type,
            such as a string too long for a fixed size char array, now dies with the error, as
            for a function argument, instead of silently leaving the variable unchanged.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Scilab] Fix reading a fixed size char array holding as many chars as it has
            elements, so with no NUL terminator, such as a char[4] member set to "abcd" in C,
            which read past the end of the array. The string read is now limited to the array
            size, as with the other target languages.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby] A director method parameter that is a reference to
            a char array, such as 'char (&)[4]', is now passed to the target language as a string,
            as for the other target languages, instead of as a pointer. In PHP this also applies to
            a fixed size char array, such as 'char [4]'.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [D, Go, OCaml] Fix the wrappers for a director method with a char array parameter,
            such as 'char [4]', 'char []' or 'char (&)[4]', which did not compile. In OCaml this
            also fixes a director method with a 'char *' parameter.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [C#, Go, Java] Fix a director method with a fixed size char array parameter, such as
            'char [4]', or a reference to one, reading past the end of an array passed from C++
            holding as many chars as it has elements, so with no NUL terminator. The string
            passed to the target language is now limited to the array size.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [C, C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, OCaml, PHP, R]
            A char array variable or member of unknown size is now read only, giving warning 462,
            as there is no size to bound a copy into it, for example:

              char name[] = "abc";
              struct Message {
                int len;
                char text[];
              };

            Setting it used strcpy, writing past the end of the array for a longer string.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Octave, Perl, Python, Ruby, Scilab, Tcl] #1699
            Fix the wrapper for a char array member of unknown size, such as a flexible array
            member, which did not compile. The member is now read only, giving warning 462, as a
            global variable of this type already was.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            A typemap for a fixed size array, such as 'int [ANY]', is no longer used for an array
            of unknown size, such as 'int []'. An [ANY] typemap can rely on the array size, such as
            by using $1_dim0, which was empty, so the code generated did not compile. A typemap
            that does not need the size can be copied for the array of unknown size, for example:

              %typemap(in) int [] = int [ANY];

            Such copies have been added for the SWIG library typemaps that do not need the size.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [R] A parameter that is an array of strings of unknown size, such as 'char *argv[]',
            is now passed as a pointer, as for other arrays of unknown size. It previously used
            the 'char *[ANY]' typemap, which passed a copy of the first string in place of the array.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Javascript, Octave, Perl, PHP, Python, Ruby] A NULL argument, such as None in
            Python, for a parameter that is a reference to a char array, or NULL assigned to a
            variable or member of that type, is now an error, as a reference cannot be NULL,
            instead of being converted to an empty string:

              int length_of(const char (&text)[8]);

            The error is the one given for a NULL reference of any other type, such as a
            TypeError in Python and PHP and a NullReferenceError in Ruby. The other target
            languages already reject NULL for such a parameter: an ArgumentNullException in C#,
            an Exception in D, wrong-type-arg in Guile, a NullPointerException in Java, a Lua
            error, Invalid_argument in OCaml and a TypeError in R and Scilab.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [C, C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, OCaml, PHP]
            Fix setting a char[1] member or global variable to a one char string, which stored
            an empty string. The char is now stored, as for the other target languages, a char[1]
            having no room for a NUL terminator.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [OCaml] signed char and unsigned char arrays are now wrapped as the other integer arrays
            are and as in the other target languages, instead of as strings: as a pointer to the
            first element by default, or as an OCaml array with the carray.i typemaps, which now
            include them. As strings, a function reading a whole fixed size array read past the end
            of a shorter string. A global variable was set with strcpy, writing past the end of the
            array for a long string. To wrap them as strings as before, apply the char array
            typemaps:

              %apply char [ANY] { signed char [ANY], unsigned char [ANY] }

            A NULL argument for a reference to a char array, such as 'char (&)[8]', now raises
            Invalid_argument instead of crashing.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Guile] Setting a fixed size char array global variable to a string that does not fit
            along with its NUL terminator is now an error, as for a parameter or member variable,
            instead of the string being silently truncated.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Go, Guile, Java, Lua, OCaml]
            Fix reading a fixed size char array holding as many chars as it has elements, so with
            no NUL terminator, such as a char[4] member set to "abcd" in C, which read past the
            end of the array. The string read is now limited to the array size, as with the other
            target languages.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Javascript] With JavaScriptCore, reading a fixed size char array holding as many
            chars as it has elements, so with no NUL terminator, such as a char[4] member set to
            "abcd" in C, read past the end of the array. The string read is now limited to the
            array size, as with the other Javascript engines.

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Scilab] A string passed or assigned to a fixed size char array parameter, member or
            global variable that does not fit along with its NUL terminator is now an error. It
            was silently truncated before. A string with as many chars as the array, such as a 16
            char string for a char[16], was copied in without a NUL terminator, so reading the array
            as a string read past its end.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [Javascript] With JavaScriptCore, a string with as many chars as a fixed size char
            array, such as a 16 char string for a char[16] parameter, member or global variable,
            was copied into the array without a NUL terminator, so reading the array as a string
            read past its end. Such a string is now an error, as it is with the other Javascript
            engines.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-05: wsfulton
            [C#, D, Go, Java, Lua, OCaml, PHP, R] Fix a function with a fixed size char array
            parameter reading past the end of the string passed to it, such as:

              void set_name(char name[16]);
              void set_name_ref(char (&name)[16]);

            The function was given a buffer only as long as the string, so reading or writing
            all 16 chars went past its end. The string is now copied into a 16 char array with
            the unused chars zero filled, as is already done for Python, Perl, Ruby, Tcl and
            others. In D, Lua, OCaml and PHP, changes the function makes to the array also no
            longer alter the target language string passed in.

            A string that does not fit along with its NUL terminator is now an error: an
            IllegalArgumentException in Java, ArgumentException in C#, Exception in D,
            ValueError in PHP, Invalid_argument in OCaml, a panic in Go and a Lua or R error. This
            includes setting a fixed size char array member or global variable, which silently
            truncated an overlong string before, except that R stored the first chars without a NUL
            terminator and OCaml wrote past the end of a global. In R, setting one to a shorter
            string also no longer copies the memory after the end of the string. The wrapper now uses
            the array size, so a size that is a constant it cannot access, such as a private class
            enum, no longer compiles, as for the other target languages.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-04: wsfulton
            Fix a named non-type template parameter of class type introduced with the 'struct',
            'union' or 'class' keyword, which was taken to be a type parameter constrained by a
            C++20 concept of that name, so overloading its template with one taking a type
            parameter made every instantiation an "Ambiguous template instantiation" error:

              template<typename T> int kind();
              template<struct Point P> int kind();
              %template(kind_value) kind<origin>;

2026-10-03: wsfulton
            Fix a universal character name, such as \u263A or \U0001F600, in a character literal or
            a narrow string literal, which was kept as the text \u263A, giving the wrong value in
            generated code, such as for a constant or default argument:

              #define SMILE L'\u263A'
              int f(wchar_t c = L'\u263A');

2026-10-03: olly
	    [Guile] Guile >= 2.0 is now required, since we haven't been testing
	    with Guile 1.x for approaching 5 years.  Some internal compatibility
	    code has been cleaned up - if you were using these in your interface
	    files, the suggested updates are:

	    * Replace `swig_guile_proc` with `scm_t_subr`
	    * Replace `guile_destructor` with `swig_guile_destructor`

2026-10-02: wsfulton
            Fix a pointer to member type-id used as the default of a template type parameter
            declared with class or typename, which was a syntax error, and a function pointer or
            other type-id with a parenthesised declarator, which was wrapped by code that did not
            compile, such as:

              template<class T = int S::*> struct P {...};
              template<class T = int (*)(int)> struct Q {...};

2026-10-02: wsfulton
            Fix the line numbers in warnings and errors when a macro passes on a multiline macro
            call in one of its arguments to another macro, such as DECLARE below. With
            -macroerrors, the warning for the unknown base class was reported two lines after its
            line in DECLARE:

              %define VAL(n)
              (n)
              %enddef

              %define DECLARE(name, n)
              const int name = VAL(n);
              struct name ## _after : public Unknown {};
              %enddef

              DECLARE(r, VAL(3))

            Without -macroerrors, the code following such a macro call was reported too many lines
            later when the macro passing on the argument is a #define macro.

2026-10-02: olly
	    [PHP] Adjust generated code to be compatible with PHP 8.6.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            [OCaml] Fix a heap overflow in the carray.i typemaps for an array parameter, which
            allocated one byte per element instead of the size of an element. In C++, an array
            of a simple type such as int or double, which is allocated with malloc, is now also
            released with free instead of delete [].

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix syntax error for sizeof applied to a member of a parenthesised expression, such as:

              const size_t n = sizeof (x).m;
              void f(size_t n = sizeof (p)->m);

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            #543 Fix syntax error for a pointer, reference or array of pointers type-id used as
            the default of a template type parameter, such as:

              template<class T = int *> struct P {...};
              template<class T1, class T2 = T1 *[2]> struct Q {...};

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            [PHP] Fix a C++11 enum class enumerator initialised by a character literal, which was
            wrapped by code that did not compile, such as:

              enum class Letter { A = 'a', B };

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix a reference to a function used as a parameter or return type, which was wrapped
            by code that did not compile, or crashed SWIG for D, such as:

              typedef int Fn(int);
              int call(int (&f)(int), int i);
              int call_typedef(Fn &f, int i);
              int (&get())(int);
              Fn &get_typedef();

            It is now wrapped in the same way as a pointer to a function, so a function pointer
            constant or the result of get() can be passed to call().

            A function returning a reference to an array, such as 'int (&get_array())[4]', is
            also parsed correctly now. As when the array type is a typedef, it returns a pointer
            to the array instead of a pointer to its first element.

            *** POTENTIAL INCOMPATIBILITY ***

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            A member function declared through a function typedef with an rvalue ref-qualifier
            is now ignored by default with warning 405, the same as when it is written out in
            full, instead of being wrapped by code that does not compile:

              typedef int Fn(int) &&;
              struct S {
                Fn f;
              };

            It can be unignored in the same way too, such as with %feature("ignore", "0") S::f;.
            Warning 405 is also no longer issued for the typedef itself.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix a possible crash when using -macroerrors, such as when a multiline macro expands
            another macro.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix the line numbers in warnings and errors for the code following a multiline
            macro expansion when using -macroerrors. They were counted from the last code before
            the macro invocation instead of from the invocation, so any blank lines, comments or
            macro definitions in between were not counted.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix Go code that does not compile when wrapping an array that has a '<'
            operator in a dimension, such as:

              int grid[LEN << 1];

            The '<' was mistaken for the start of a template argument list, so the array was
            wrapped as an unknown type and an invalid 'type int interface' was generated.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix an enum value that is a wide character literal failing with "Type error.
            Expecting an integral type". It is now accepted, the same as a plain character
            literal:

              enum E { A = L'w', B = L'\x263A' };

            Also fix the value of a wide character literal holding a character that is not
            ASCII, such as L'\x263A' or the character written as itself, used for a constant
            or default argument. It was truncated to a single byte, or split into the bytes of
            its UTF-8 encoding.

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            A wide raw string literal, such as LR"(text)", is now parsed as a wide string literal,
            the same as L"text", instead of as a narrow string literal. The generated code for a
            constant or a C++11 auto variable initialised by one no longer uses a narrow string,
            which did not compile, and a compact default argument no longer passes a narrow string
            where a wide string is expected:

              %constant const wchar_t *C = LR"(text)";
              auto v = LR"(text)";
              %feature("compactdefaultargs") f;
              void f(const wchar_t *s = LR"(text)");

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            [Go] Fix the generated code failing to compile for a constant initialised by a wide
            string literal:

              %constant const wchar_t *C = L"text";

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            Fix a crash wrapping a reference to a function declared through a function typedef:

              typedef int Fn(int);
              Fn &fnref = fn;

            The reference is now wrapped as a function, the same as a reference to a function
            declared without a typedef. A member function declared through a const qualified
            function typedef is also fixed, as its wrapper did not compile:

              typedef int CFn(int) const;
              struct S {
                CFn f;
              };

2026-10-01: wsfulton
            [Javascript] Fix a member function declared through a function typedef, which was
            registered without its wrapper function, so the generated code did not compile:

              typedef int Fn(int);
              struct S {
                Fn f;
              };

